var e=JSON.parse('[[601,"The HTML Custom Elements API lets developers create their own HTML elements, which behave like the standard elements but can have their own unique logic and style. To create a custom element you have to define a class that inherits from `HTMLElement` and register it by means of `customElements.define()`. These elements can be used on a page in just the same way as the standard HTML tags. Custom Elements extend the capabilities of HTML, making it more flexible for modern web applications.","The HTML Custom Elements API is part of the Web Components specification, which lets developers create their own HTML elements with custom logic and behaviour. Custom Elements let you define new types of element that can be reused anywhere in an application, improving the structure and the modularity of the code.\\nCreating a custom element:\\nTo create a custom element you have to carry out three steps:\\n\\n- Create a class inheriting from `HTMLElement`:\\n\\n- Register the element in the browser by means of `customElements.define()`:\\n\\n- Use the custom element on the page:\\n\\nOnce registered, the `my-custom-element` element can be used anywhere on an HTML page, like an ordinary tag.\\nThe advantages of custom elements:\\n\\n- The encapsulation of the logic: The logic of the element\'s behaviour is fully isolated in the class, which makes the code more organised and modular.\\n\\n- Reuse: Having created a custom element, you can use it many times in different parts of an application, which reduces the duplication of code.\\n\\nCustom Elements are a tool for creating reusable components in web applications. They make HTML more flexible by letting developers define new elements with unique functions and behaviour.","class MyCustomElement extends HTMLElement {    \\n  constructor() {        \\n    super();        \\n    this.innerHTML = \\"<p>This is a custom element</p>\\";    \\n  } \\n}\\n\\ncustomElements.define(\'my-custom-element\', MyCustomElement);\\n\\n<my-custom-element></my-custom-element>"],[602,"The `tabindex` attribute controls the order in which elements on a page receive focus during keyboard navigation. Elements with a positive `tabindex` value receive focus in ascending order of the number, elements with `tabindex=\\"0\\"` in the order in which they appear, and negative values make an element inaccessible to focusing from the keyboard. The `focus()` method in JavaScript lets you set focus on an element programmatically. That matters for ensuring accessibility and convenient control for users with special needs.","Focus on a web page is the mechanism that determines which element is active and ready for interaction with the user. That matters for navigating the page from the keyboard and for ensuring the accessibility of web content for people with disabilities.\\nThe `tabindex` attribute:\\nThe `tabindex` attribute controls the order in which elements on the page will receive focus when the `Tab` key is used. The values of `tabindex` can be:\\n\\n- Positive values: Elements with a positive `tabindex` receive focus first, in ascending order of the value. An element with `tabindex=\\"1\\"`, for example, will receive focus before an element with `tabindex=\\"2\\"`.\\n\\n- The value `0`: Elements with `tabindex=\\"0\\"` take part in the standard focus navigation but have no priority.\\n\\n- Negative values: Elements with a negative `tabindex`, such as `tabindex=\\"-1\\"`, cannot be focused from the keyboard, but focus can be set on them programmatically by means of JavaScript.\\n\\nAn example of the use of `tabindex`:\\n\\nIn this example the focus order will be: Button 1, Button 2, Button 3, since `tabindex` determines the sequence.\\nThe `focus()` method:\\nIn JavaScript the `focus()` method lets you set focus on an element programmatically. That is useful for managing focus when the interface changes or when the user\'s attention has to be drawn to a particular element:\\n\\nAn example of the use of `focus()` to improve accessibility: When the user closes a modal window, focus can be returned to the element it was opened from:\\n\\nManaging focus by means of `tabindex` and methods such as `focus()` matters for ensuring accessibility and improving the user experience. That is especially critical for users who rely on the keyboard to navigate pages, such as people with disabilities.","<button tabindex=\\"1\\">Button 1</button> \\n<button tabindex=\\"3\\">Button 3</button> \\n<button tabindex=\\"2\\">Button 2</button>\\n\\ndocument.getElementById(\'myButton\').focus();\\n\\nbutton.addEventListener(\'click\', function() {\\n  modal.close();    \\n  button.focus(); // We return focus to the button after closing \\n});"],[603,"HTML5 offers new tags and functions that are not always supported by older browsers. So that pages work correctly in such browsers, fallback mechanisms are used — alternative content or tags that are picked up if the main functionality is unavailable. For video, for example, you can use the `<object>` element or a link for downloading the file if `<video>` is not supported. For cross-browser compatibility, polyfills and shims are also applied — scripts that add support for modern functions in older browsers.","Fallback mechanisms and cross-browser compatibility are important aspects of development, which ensure the availability of web pages\' functionality in different browsers, including older versions.\\nAn example of a fallback for `<video>`:\\n\\nIn this example, if the browser does not support `<video>`, the user will see the text and a link for downloading the video.\\nA fallback for CSS:\\nIf a CSS property is not supported, you can use obsolete or alternative styles:\\n\\nCross-browser compatibility:\\nTo achieve compatibility the following are often applied:\\n\\n- Polyfills: These are scripts that add support for modern functions in older browsers. A polyfill for `fetch()` or `Promise`, for example.\\n\\n- Shims: Similar to polyfills, but they replace unavailable functions with alternative ones.\\n\\nAn example of a polyfill for `fetch()`: If the browser does not support `fetch()`, you can use a polyfill:\\n\\nTechniques for cross-browser compatibility:\\n\\n- Progressive Enhancement: Basic functionality for everyone, enhanced functionality for newer browsers.\\n\\n- Graceful Degradation: Functions for modern browsers are developed first, and then fallback mechanisms are added for older ones.\\n\\n- The use of feature detection: Instead of checking the browser\'s version, specific capabilities are checked:\\n\\nThe use of fallback mechanisms and polyfills matters for ensuring that web pages work on devices with differing support for modern standards. It helps make a site available to a larger number of users.","<video controls>  \\n  <source src=\\"video.mp4\\" type=\\"video/mp4\\">  \\n  <source src=\\"video.ogv\\" type=\\"video/ogg\\">  Your browser does not support the video tag.  \\n  <a href=\\"video.mp4\\">Download the video</a> \\n</video>\\n\\n.example {  \\n  background: rgb(255, 0, 0); /* the old syntax */  \\n  background: linear-gradient(red, yellow); /* the new syntax */ \\n}\\n\\n<script src=\\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/fetch/2.0.4/fetch.min.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\nif (\'querySelector\' in document) {    \\n  // code for modern browsers \\n}"],[604,"Lazy loading is a technique that lets you postpone the loading of images or frames until they appear in the visible area of the screen. The `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute in the `<img>` or `<iframe>` tag postpones the loading of the element automatically. That shortens the initial loading time of the page and reduces the use of traffic. Alternative methods include the use of the Intersection Observer API for more flexible control over the loading of elements.","Lazy loading is a method of optimising performance that lets you load resources only when the user needs them — that is, when they fall into the visible area of the screen (the viewport). That is useful for pages with a large number of images or videos, where loading all of the resources at once can slow down the initial loading of the page.\\nThe `loading` attribute:\\nHTML5 added native support for lazy loading for the `<img>` and `<iframe>` elements by means of the `loading` attribute.\\n\\nWhen this attribute is used, the browser loads the image automatically only when it is going to be in the visible area of the screen.\\nThe advantages of `loading=\\"lazy\\"`:\\n\\n- Simplicity of use: Adding the attribute requires minimal changes in the code.\\n\\n- Improved performance: It speeds up the rendering of the page, since it reduces the number of resources loaded.\\n\\n- Browser support: Modern browsers support this attribute, which makes it available for use without third-party libraries.\\n\\nAlternative methods:\\nThe Intersection Observer API: This API provides more control over when elements should be loaded. It tracks when an element falls into the visible area and starts the loading of the resource.\\n\\nPostponed loading by means of JavaScript: You can postpone the loading by hand, changing the value of the `src` attribute of images or `<iframe>` elements only when they become visible.\\nLazy loading helps shorten the loading time of a page and save the user\'s traffic. Although the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute is a convenient and simple solution, more complex scenarios can be implemented by means of the Intersection Observer for a fine tuning of the loading process.","<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"Lazy loaded image\\" loading=\\"lazy\\">\\n\\nconst img = document.querySelector(\'img\'); \\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) => {  \\n  entries.forEach(entry => {    \\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {      \\n      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;      \\n      observer.unobserve(entry.target);    \\n    }  \\n  }); \\n}); \\nobserver.observe(img);"],[605,"HTML Imports was an experimental technology that let you load and include HTML files in other documents by means of the `<link>` tag. It was rejected because of weak support and the presence of alternative standards such as JavaScript modules and Web Components. Modern alternatives include ES modules (import/export) and technologies based on templates and components, such as React and Web Components.","HTML Imports was proposed as a way of including HTML code modularly in other documents. By means of the `<link>` tag you could load an external HTML file and include its content in the page:\\n\\nThis technology did not gain wide recognition, however, and was rejected for a number of reasons:\\n\\n- Limited browser support: HTML Imports was supported only in some browsers, such as Chrome, which made it unsuitable for cross-browser projects.\\n\\n- Difficulties with performance: Importing HTML files could lead to an increase in the loading time and in the complexity of managing dependencies.\\n\\n- Better alternatives: The ECMAScript (ES6) standard proposed more universal modules based on JavaScript, which cope better with the task of separating and reusing code.\\n\\nModern alternatives:\\n\\n- ES modules (JavaScript): This is a standardised way of organising and loading code. By means of the `import/export` directive you can divide code into modules that are easy to connect to one another.\\n\\n- Web Components: Components based on the Web Components standard let you create custom elements containing HTML, CSS and JavaScript, which makes them an excellent alternative for building a modular interface.\\n\\nHTML Imports thus did not gain support because of the appearance of more advanced standards, such as ES modules and Web Components, which provide the flexibility and the power for building modular applications.","<link rel=\\"import\\" href=\\"header.html\\">\\n\\n// module.js \\nexport const myFunction = () => { console.log(\\"Hello, World!\\"); }; \\n// main.js \\nimport { myFunction } from \'./module.js\'; \\nmyFunction();\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends HTMLElement {  \\n  connectedCallback() {    \\n    this.innerHTML = `<p>Custom component content</p>`;  \\n  } \\n} \\ncustomElements.define(\'my-component\', MyComponent);"],[606,"The optimisation of rendering includes minimising reflow operations (the rebuilding of the document\'s structure) and repaints (the updating of visual changes). That is achieved by reducing the frequency of changes to the DOM and by using the buffering of changes through `requestAnimationFrame` and CSS transforms, which do not cause a reflow. It also matters to reduce the number of heavy operations, such as changing the size and the position of elements.","The process of rendering HTML includes several stages: building the DOM tree, the render tree, computing the styles and applying them, and also the processes of reflow and repainting.\\n\\n- Reflow: This is the process in which the browser recomputes the position and the size of every element on the page when the DOM or the CSS changes. It is one of the most expensive operations.\\n\\n- Repaint: This is the process of updating the visible part of the page without changing its structure (changing the background colour, for example).\\n\\nFrequent reflows and repaints can slow a page down substantially, especially on mobile devices with low performance.\\nMethods of optimisation:\\n\\n- Minimising changes in the DOM: Changing a large number of elements in the DOM individually causes a reflow and a repaint for every change. It is better to group the changes or to use DOM fragments in order to insert several elements at once.\\n\\n- The use of CSS transforms: Changes through `transform` and `opacity` do not usually cause a reflow, since they do not require the position of the elements to be recomputed.\\n\\n- Buffering the changes by means of `requestAnimationFrame`: That lets the browser carry out the updates before the next repaint of the screen, preventing frequent repaints.\\n\\n- Avoiding reading properties that cause a reflow: Properties such as `offsetHeight` or `clientWidth` cause a reflow, since the browser has to recompute their values. Instead, try to cache the results or to use such properties less often.\\n\\nMinimising reflows and repaints can thus substantially raise a page\'s performance, especially when working with dynamic content and animations.","const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment(); \\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {  \\n  const div = document.createElement(\'div\');  \\n  fragment.appendChild(div); \\n} \\ndocument.body.appendChild(fragment);\\n\\n.animate {  transform: translateX(100px); /* A fast animation without a reflow */ }\\n\\nlet element = document.getElementById(\'myElement\'); \\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {  element.style.left = \'100px\'; });"],[607,"For multilingual support in HTML the `lang` and `dir` attributes are used. The `lang` attribute states the language of the content (`lang=\\"en\\"` for English, for example), while `dir` states the direction of the text (`dir=\\"rtl\\"`, for example, for languages that are read from right to left). To manage dynamic localisation, JavaScript and libraries of the i18next kind are often used, which let you change the language of the interface depending on the user\'s preferences.","Multilingual support (or internationalisation, i18n) lets you display the content of a web page in several languages, in order to make the site available to users from different countries. The main techniques include the use of HTML attributes and of third-party tools for changing the language dynamically.\\nThe `lang` attribute: This attribute is used to state the language of an element\'s content or of the whole page. It helps search engines, and also screen readers, interpret the content correctly.\\n\\nThe `dir` attribute: Sets the direction of the text. For Arabic or Hebrew, for example, `dir=\\"rtl\\"` (right-to-left) is used, which changes the direction of the text on the page.\\n\\nLocalisation using JavaScript: To change the language dynamically depending on the user\'s choice or on the browser\'s settings, you can use libraries of the i18next kind. These libraries let you hold the translations in JSON files and substitute the corresponding values into the page.\\n\\nFor more complex applications, dynamic localisation is often applied, where on the basis of the user\'s settings the language and the direction of the text can change in real time.","<html lang=\\"en\\">  \\n  <body>    \\n    <p lang=\\"fr\\">Bonjour!</p> <!-- This paragraph is in French -->  \\n  </body> \\n</html>\\n\\n<p dir=\\"rtl\\">هذا نص عربي</p> <!-- Text in Arabic -->\\n\\nimport i18next from \'i18next\'; \\ni18next.init({  \\n  resources: {    \\n    en: { translation: { \\"welcome\\": \\"Welcome\\" } },    \\n    fr: { translation: { \\"welcome\\": \\"Bienvenue\\" } }  \\n  },  \\n  lng: \\"en\\", // The default language \\n}); \\ndocument.getElementById(\'welcome\').innerText = i18next.t(\'welcome\');"],[608,"The `<canvas>` element is used to draw graphics on a web page by means of JavaScript. With it you can create both simple shapes and complex animations and games. It has no built-in content, and all of the graphical elements are drawn dynamically through methods such as `fillRect()` for drawing rectangles and `beginPath()` for creating lines and curves.","The `<canvas>` element is an HTML5 element that provides a place for drawing using JavaScript. Unlike other HTML elements, `<canvas>` does not display content directly but represents a container in which graphics can be drawn by means of scripts. To work with it the `CanvasRenderingContext2D` API is used, which provides methods for drawing shapes, lines, text and images.\\nAn example of the simple use of `<canvas>`:\\n\\nBy means of the context\'s methods you can draw both simple forms and more complex elements:\\n\\n- Rectangles: `fillRect(x, y, width, height)` draws a filled rectangle.\\n\\n- Lines and curves: To create a path, `beginPath()` is used, after which you can add lines through `moveTo()` and `lineTo()`.\\n\\nAn example of drawing a circle:\\n\\nScenarios of use:\\n\\n- Games and animations: `<canvas>` is often used to create games, since it lets you update the graphics quickly and supports dynamic interaction.\\n\\n- The visualisation of data: It is also applied to create graphs and diagrams.\\n\\n- Editing images: By means of `<canvas>` you can manipulate images (cropping, filters and so on, for example).\\n\\nThe `<canvas>` element is a powerful tool for creating interactive graphics in the browser, providing a broad set of methods for working with 2D content.","<canvas id=\\"myCanvas\\" width=\\"200\\" height=\\"200\\"></canvas> \\n<script>  \\n  const canvas = document.getElementById(\'myCanvas\');  \\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext(\'2d\');  // We draw a rectangle  \\n  ctx.fillStyle = \'green\';  \\n  ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 150, 100); \\n<\/script>\\n\\nctx.beginPath(); \\nctx.arc(100, 100, 50, 0, Math.PI * 2); \\nctx.stroke();"],[609,"The HTML5 History API lets you change the URL in the browser without reloading the page, preserving the history of the transitions. The API\'s main methods are `pushState()`, `replaceState()` and the `popstate` event. They let you change the state of the page dynamically, updating the URL, which makes navigation in single-page applications (SPAs) smoother and more interactive.","The HTML5 History API is a set of methods for managing the navigation history in the browser without reloading the page. In traditional web applications every transition through a link leads to a new page being loaded. By means of the History API you can change the content and the URL of a page dynamically without fully reloading it, which makes the interaction smoother, especially in single-page applications (SPAs).\\nThe main methods of the History API:\\n\\n- `history.pushState(state, title, url)`: Adds a new item to the browser\'s history with an updated URL. This method does not reload the page, but it lets you change the address bar.\\n\\n- `history.replaceState(state, title, url)`: Replaces the current item of the history without adding a new one, which is useful when you have to change the URL but not save a new step in the history.\\n\\n- `window.onpopstate`: This event fires when moving backwards or forwards in the history. It can be used to update the content of the page during such transitions.\\n\\nThe History API is actively used in modern frameworks such as React and Vue in order to manage routing in an SPA. That lets you make navigation more interactive and speed up the application\'s response.","history.pushState({page: 1}, \\"Title\\", \\"/new-url\\");\\n\\nhistory.replaceState({page: 2}, \\"Title\\", \\"/another-url\\");\\n\\nwindow.onpopstate = function(event) {  \\n  console.log(\\"location: \\" + document.location + \\", state: \\" + JSON.stringify(event.state)); \\n};"],[610,"A dark theme can be implemented by means of the `prefers-color-scheme` CSS media query, which determines the user\'s preferences as regards the colour scheme (light or dark). Depending on the browser\'s preferences, the corresponding styles are applied. You can also set up switching the theme by hand by means of JavaScript and CSS classes.","Support for a dark theme in web applications lets users switch automatically between a light and a dark theme on the basis of their system settings. That is achieved by means of the `prefers-color-scheme` CSS media query. This media query checks which colour scheme the user\'s system prefers (light or dark) and applies the corresponding styles.\\nAn example of the use of the media query for a dark theme:\\n\\nThat lets you apply a dark theme automatically if the user\'s device is set to a dark colour scheme.\\nFor manual switching between the themes by means of a button you can use JavaScript. You can hold the state of the theme in `localStorage`, for example, and change the classes in the DOM dynamically.\\nAn example with JavaScript for switching the theme by hand:\\n\\nBy means of this code you can save the user\'s choice and apply it on their next visit to the page.\\nYou can also use meta tags, such as `theme-color`, to change the browser\'s colour scheme:\\n\\nThese meta tags set the colour scheme of the browser\'s interface depending on the theme. Support for a dark theme makes web applications more adaptive and improves the user experience.","/* The default style (the light theme) */ \\nbody {  \\n  background-color: white;  \\n  color: black; \\n} \\n/* The dark theme */ \\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {  \\n  body {    \\n    background-color: black;    \\n    color: white;  \\n  } \\n}\\n\\nconst toggleTheme = () => {  \\n  const theme = document.body.classList.toggle(\'dark\') ? \'dark\' : \'light\';    \\n  localStorage.setItem(\'theme\', theme); \\n}; \\n  \\ndocument.addEventListener(\'DOMContentLoaded\', () => {  \\n  const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem(\'theme\');  \\n  if (savedTheme) {    \\n    document.body.classList.add(savedTheme);  \\n  } \\n});\\n\\n<meta name=\\"theme-color\\" content=\\"#000000\\" media=\\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\\"> \\n<meta name=\\"theme-color\\" content=\\"#ffffff\\" media=\\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\\">"],[611,"In JavaScript variables can be declared by means of `var`, `let` and `const`. `var` is the obsolete way; it has peculiarities such as hoisting and function scope. `let` and `const` appeared in ES6 and have block scope. `const` is used for variables that cannot be reassigned.","In JavaScript there are three ways of declaring a variable: `var`, `let` and `const`.\\n\\n- `var` is the old way of declaring, which has function scope and supports hoisting. That means that variables declared with `var` can be used before they are actually declared, which sometimes leads to unexpected results.\\n\\n- `let` was introduced in ES6 and replaces `var` in most cases. It has block scope — that is, the variable is available only inside the `{}` block in which it is declared. That makes the code more predictable.\\n\\n- `const` also appeared in ES6 and is used to declare constants whose values cannot be reassigned after initialisation. That does not mean, however, that an object declared with `const` cannot change — it is only the reference to the object itself that cannot be changed.",null],[612,"`var` has function scope, supports hoisting and can be reassigned. `let` has block scope and can also be reassigned. `const` also has block scope, but its value cannot be reassigned.","- `var` — variables declared through `var` have scope at the level of the function. That means they are visible throughout the function, even if they are declared in blocks such as loops. Besides that, `var` supports hoisting, which lets you access the variable before its declaration, although its value will be `undefined` if nothing had been assigned to it before that.\\n\\n- `let` is a more modern way of declaring variables, which has block scope. That means the variable exists only inside the block where it is declared. `let` is not hoisted the way `var` is, which makes the behaviour more predictable.\\n\\n- `const` — like `let`, it has block scope, but it differs in that the variable\'s value cannot be reassigned. That is used for values that should not change, such as references to objects or primitives.\\n\\nAn example:","if (true) {    \\n  var a = 1;   // available everywhere in the function    \\n  let b = 2;   // available only inside the block    \\n  const c = 3; // also available only inside the block \\n}"],[613,"In JavaScript there are eight data types: `string`, `number`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint`. There is also the `object` type, which includes arrays, functions and objects.","In JavaScript there are the following data types:\\nThe primitive types:\\n\\n- `string` — for string data (`\\"Hello\\"`, for example).\\n\\n- `number` — for numbers, both whole and floating-point (`42`, `3.14`, for example).\\n\\n- `boolean` — for the logical values `true` and `false`.\\n\\n- `null` — a special value denoting \\"nothing\\".\\n\\n- `undefined` — the value assigned to a variable by default if no other value has been assigned to it.\\n\\n- `symbol` — unique and immutable identifiers, introduced in ES6.\\n\\n- `bigint` — for working with whole numbers of arbitrary length\\n\\nThe `object` type is a more complex structure, which can hold collections of data or more complex entities, such as arrays, functions and objects.\\nAn example:","let name = \\"Alice\\";      // string \\nlet age = 25;            // number \\nlet isStudent = true;    // boolean \\nlet course = null;       // null \\nlet address;             // undefined \\nlet id = Symbol(\'id\');   // symbol \\nlet bigNumber = 123456789012345678901234567890n; // bigint\\nlet user = { name, age };// object"],[614,"In JavaScript the result of calling `typeof null` is \\"object\\". That is a well-known error in the language, since `null` is not in fact an object. This behaviour has remained in the language for historical reasons.","The `typeof` operator is used to determine the data type of a variable. In JavaScript there is one well-known error, however — when `typeof null` is called the result will be \\"object\\". This behaviour is explained by a historical peculiarity of the language. Although `null` is technically not an object, its type is returned as \\"object\\". In fact `null` is a primitive data type, which is used to denote the absence of any value.\\nAn example:\\n\\nAlthough this behaviour may seem counter-intuitive, it matters to know about it when working with JavaScript.","console.log(typeof null); // \\"object\\""],[615,"`NaN` (Not-a-Number) is a special value representing the result of a non-numeric operation that ought to return a number. `NaN` is of the `number` type, but it is not equal to any value, not even to itself. To check whether a value is NaN, the `isNaN()` or `Number.isNaN()` function is used.","`NaN` (Not-a-Number) is a value in JavaScript that appears when an operation intended for working with numbers returns an incorrect result. For example, when dividing a string by a number or when attempting to convert incorrect data into a number. Despite the fact that NaN denotes \\"not a number\\", its type in JavaScript is `number`.\\nA peculiarity of NaN is that it is not equal even to itself. That means the check `NaN === NaN` always returns `false`. To determine whether a value is NaN, the `isNaN()` and `Number.isNaN()` functions are used. `isNaN()` attempts to convert the value into a number before the check, whereas `Number.isNaN()` strictly checks whether the value is exactly NaN.\\nAn example:","console.log(NaN === NaN);          // false \\nconsole.log(isNaN(\\"hello\\"));       // true, because a string cannot be converted into a number\\nconsole.log(Number.isNaN(NaN));    // true \\nconsole.log(Number.isNaN(\\"hello\\"));// false, because this is not NaN but simply a string"],[616,"Unary operators are operators that work with a single operand. In JavaScript they include: the unary plus (`+`), the unary minus (`-`), the increment operator (`++`), the decrement operator (`--`), the logical NOT operator (`!`), the typeof operator and the delete operator.","Unary operators are operators that are applied to a single operand. In JavaScript there are several unary operators:\\nThe unary plus (`+`): used to convert the operand into a number.\\n\\nThe unary minus (`-`): converts the operand into a negative number.\\n\\nThe increment (`++`): increases the value of a variable by one. It can be prefix or postfix.\\n\\nThe decrement (`--`): decreases the value of a variable by one. It too can be prefix or postfix.\\n\\nThe logical NOT (`!`): converts a value into its opposite logical value (true becomes false and vice versa).\\n\\n`typeof`: returns a string denoting the type of the operand.\\n\\n`delete`: deletes a property of an object.\\n\\nThese operators let you change the value of variables, check their types or carry out logical operations.","let a = \\"5\\"; \\nconsole.log(+a); // 5\\n\\nlet b = 10; \\nconsole.log(-b); // -10\\n\\nlet c = 5; \\nconsole.log(++c); // 6 (prefix) \\nconsole.log(c++); // 6 (postfix, it first returns and then increases)\\n\\nlet d = 5; \\nconsole.log(--d); // 4 (prefix) \\nconsole.log(d--); // 4 (postfix)\\n\\nlet e = true; \\nconsole.log(!e); // false\\n\\nconsole.log(typeof 123); // \\"number\\"\\n\\nlet obj = { name: \\"Alice\\" }; \\ndelete obj.name; \\nconsole.log(obj.name); // undefined"],[617,"Binary operators work with two operands. They include the arithmetic operators (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`), the comparison operators (`==`, `===`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`), the logical operators (`&&`, `||`), the bitwise operators (`&`, `|`, `^`, `>>`, `<<`), and also the assignment operator (`=`).","Binary operators require two operands in order to carry out an operation. In JavaScript there are several categories of binary operator:\\nThe arithmetic operators: they carry out mathematical operations.\\n\\n- `+` (addition)\\n\\n- `-` (subtraction)\\n\\n- `*` (multiplication)\\n\\n- `/` (division)\\n\\n- `%` (the remainder of a division)\\n\\nThe comparison operators: they are used to compare values.\\n\\n- `==` (loose equality)\\n\\n- `===` (strict equality)\\n\\n- `!=` (inequality)\\n\\n- `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` (less than, greater than, less than or equal to, greater than or equal to)\\n\\nThe logical operators: they work with logical (boolean) values.\\n\\n- `&&` (logical AND)\\n\\n- `||` (logical OR)\\n\\nThe bitwise operators: they work at the level of the bits of numbers.\\n\\n- `&` (bitwise AND)\\n\\n- `|` (bitwise OR)\\n\\n- `^` (bitwise exclusive OR)\\n\\n- `>>`, `<<` (shift right, shift left)\\n\\nThe assignment operator: assigns a value to a variable.\\n\\n- `=` (assignment)\\n\\nBinary operators are the foundation of many operations in JavaScript and are used to carry out mathematical operations, compare values and work with logic.","let a = 5, b = 2; \\nconsole.log(a + b); // 7 \\nconsole.log(a * b); // 10\\n\\nconsole.log(5 == \'5\'); // true (loose comparison) \\nconsole.log(5 === \'5\'); // false (strict comparison)\\n\\nlet x = true, y = false; \\nconsole.log(x && y); // false \\nconsole.log(x || y); // true\\n\\nconsole.log(5 & 1); // 1 (in the binary system: 0101 & 0001 = 0001)\\n\\nlet z = 10;"],[618,"The ternary operator is a short way of writing the `if...else` conditional statement. The syntax looks like this: `condition ? expression1 : expression2`. If the condition is true, `expression1` is carried out; if it is false, `expression2`.","The ternary operator (or conditional operator) in JavaScript lets you simplify the writing of an ordinary `if...else` condition.\\nThat is useful for short, simple conditions where you have to choose one of two values. The syntax of the ternary operator:\\n\\nIf the condition is true, the first expression is carried out; if it is false, the second. For example:\\n\\nThis code is equivalent to the following:\\n\\nThe ternary operator is often used to simplify simple conditions, especially when assigning values to variables or in JSX when working with React.","condition ? expression1 : expression2;\\n\\nlet age = 18; \\nlet message = age >= 18 ? \\"Adult\\" : \\"Underage\\"; \\nconsole.log(message); // \\"Adult\\"\\n\\nif (age >= 18) {  \\n  message = \\"Adult\\"; \\n} else {  \\n  message = \\"Underage\\"; \\n}"],[619,"Type conversion in JavaScript can be automatic (implicit) or explicit. Automatic conversion happens when JavaScript attempts to bring one value to the type required (a string to a number, for example). Explicit conversion is carried out by means of functions such as `Number()`, `String()` or `Boolean()`.","In JavaScript type conversion is the process of bringing values of one type to another. There are two kinds of conversion: implicit (automatic) and explicit.\\nImplicit (automatic) conversion: JavaScript converts data types automatically when carrying out operations. If you add a string to a number, for example, JavaScript will convert the number into a string.\\n\\nExplicit conversion: The programmer themselves sets how and when to convert a data type by means of functions:\\n`Number()` — converts a value into a number.\\n`String()` — converts a value into a string.\\n`Boolean()` — converts a value into a boolean.\\nExamples of explicit conversion:\\n\\nIt matters to understand the difference between these kinds of conversion, since implicit conversion can lead to unexpected results in code, especially during complex operations.","let result = \'5\' + 3; // \\"53\\", the number 3 is converted into a string\\n\\nlet str = \\"123\\"; \\nlet num = Number(str); // We convert the string into a number \\nconsole.log(num); // 123 \\nlet bool = Boolean(1); // We convert the number into a boolean \\nconsole.log(bool); // true"],[620,"The comparison operators in JavaScript include: `==` (loose equality), `===` (strict equality), `!=` (loose inequality), `!==` (strict inequality), `<` (less than), `>` (greater than), `<=` (less than or equal to), `>=` (greater than or equal to). They return a boolean value: `true` or `false`, depending on the result of the comparison.","In JavaScript the comparison operators are used to match values against one another. They let you check whether values are equal, and how one value relates to another. The main operators:\\nEquality and inequality:\\n\\n- `==` (loose equality): compares values without taking the types into account. An example: `5 == \'5\'` will return `true`, since type conversion happens.\\n\\n- `===` (strict equality): compares values taking the types into account. An example: `5 === \'5\'` will return `false`, since one operand is a number and the other a string.\\n\\n- `!=` (loose inequality): checks whether values are not equal without taking the types into account. An example: `5 != \'6\'` will return `true`.\\n\\n- `!==` (strict inequality): checks whether values are not equal taking the types into account. An example: `5 !== \'5\'` will return `true`.\\n\\nThe comparison of magnitudes:\\n\\n- `<` (less than),\\n\\n- `>` (greater than),\\n\\n- `<=` (less than or equal to),\\n\\n- `>=` (greater than or equal to) — used to compare numbers or strings.\\n\\nThese operators are widely used to check conditions in loops, functions and `if...else` expressions, in order to make a program\'s logic flexible and dynamic.","console.log(3 > 2); // true \\nconsole.log(\'a\' < \'b\'); // true (comparison by Unicode)"],[621,"Comparison operators always return a boolean value: `true` or `false`. If the operator\'s condition is met (the numbers are equal or one is less than the other, for example), the result will be `true`. If the condition is not met, the result will be `false`.","In JavaScript comparison operators always return a boolean value — either `true` or `false`. Regardless of what exactly is being compared, the result of the operation will be one of these two values. An example:\\n\\nIf an equality operator is used, `==` or `===` for example, JavaScript checks whether the values are equal. If they are, the result will be `true`; if not, `false`. An example:\\n\\nThe boolean values returned by comparison operators are often used in conditions in order to control a program\'s logic. They can control the execution of blocks of code inside conditional constructions (`if`, `while`), for example.","console.log(5 > 3); // true, because 5 is greater than 3\\nconsole.log(5 < 3); // false, because 5 is not less than 3\\n\\nconsole.log(5 == \'5\'); // true (loose equality, type coercion happens)\\nconsole.log(5 === \'5\'); // false (strict equality, the types differ)"],[622,"In JavaScript the comparison of types depends on the operator. The `===` operator (strict equality) requires the types of the values being compared to be identical. The `==` operator (loose equality) automatically brings the types to a common value before the comparison. With a loose comparison, therefore, a number and a string can be equal, for example.","In JavaScript, when comparing values of different types it matters to take into account which operator is used:\\n\\n- Strict equality (`===`): this operator compares both the values and their types. If the data types differ, the result will be `false`, even if the values are identical.\\n\\n- Loose equality (`==`): before the comparison this operator attempts to bring the different data types to a single type. The string `\\"5\\"` can be converted into the number `5` for the comparison, for example.\\n\\n- Type coercion during comparison: When loose equality is used, JavaScript carries out the conversion of types automatically. That can be useful in some cases, but it can also lead to unexpected results. The empty string `\\"\\"`, for example, when compared with the number `0` will give `true`, since both variables are brought to the number `0`:\\n\\n- The comparison of objects and arrays: When objects or arrays are compared, their references in memory are compared rather than the values themselves. That means that even if two objects have identical content, they will not be equal if they are different objects\\n\\nThe comparison of types is an important part of working with JavaScript, especially when developing complex applications, where it matters to handle different data types correctly.","console.log(5 === \'5\'); // false (a number and a string) \\nconsole.log(5 === 5);   // true (both are numbers)\\n\\nconsole.log(5 == \'5\'); // true (the string \'5\' is brought to a number)\\n\\nconsole.log(\\"\\" == 0); // true\\n\\nlet a = [1, 2, 3]; \\nlet b = [1, 2, 3]; \\nconsole.log(a == b); // false (different references)"],[623,"The main logical operators in JavaScript are `&&` (logical AND), `||` (logical OR) and `!` (logical NOT). The `&&` operator returns `true` if both operands are true, the `||` operator returns `true` if at least one operand is true, and `!` returns the opposite boolean value.","Logical operators are used to carry out operations with boolean values (true/false), and also to control the flow of a program\'s execution.\\n\\n- `&&` (AND): Returns `true` if both operands are true, otherwise `false`. For example:\\n\\nUsed to check whether both conditions are met.\\n\\n- `||` (OR): Returns `true` if at least one of the operands is true. For example:\\n\\nApplied if at least one of the conditions has to be met.\\n\\n- `!` (NOT): Inverts a boolean value. If an expression is true, it will become false and vice versa. For example:\\n\\nUseful for checking opposite conditions.\\nLogical operators are widely applied in conditional constructions, in `if` expressions for example, or to control a loop when several conditions have to be checked at once.","console.log(true && true); // true \\nconsole.log(true && false); // false\\n\\nconsole.log(true || false); // true \\nconsole.log(false || false); // false\\n\\nconsole.log(!true); // false \\nconsole.log(!false); // true"],[624,"The nullish coalescing operator (`??`) returns the first operand if it is not equal to `null` or `undefined`; otherwise it returns the second operand. That is useful when you have to set a default value for a variable that may be undefined or equal to `null`.","The nullish coalescing operator (`??`) in JavaScript lets you set a default value if a variable has the value `null` or `undefined`. It differs from the `||` operator, which returns the second operand if the first is \\"falsy\\" (an empty string or `0`, for example). An example of its use:\\n\\nIn this example `name` has the value `null`, so the default value \\"Anonymous\\" will be used. If `name` had contained a string, that would have been used.\\nThe nullish coalescing operator is useful when you have to handle precisely `null` and `undefined` only, without touching other \\"falsy\\" values such as the empty string `\\"\\"`, the number `0` or `false`.","let name = null; \\nlet defaultName = name ?? \\"Anonymous\\"; \\nconsole.log(defaultName); // \\"Anonymous\\""],[625,"Loops let you repeat the execution of one and the same block of code several times while a given condition is met. In JavaScript the main loops are `for`, `while` and `do...while`. They are used when you have to go through an array of data or carry out one and the same action many times.","Loops in JavaScript are tools for the repeated execution of a block of code while a condition is met. They let you avoid repeating identical code and make processing data simpler.\\n\\n- `for`: This loop is used most often when it is known how many times the code has to be carried out. To go through an array, for example:\\n\\n- `while`: The loop is carried out while the condition is true. It is useful when the number of iterations is not known in advance:\\n\\n- `do...while`: This loop first carries out the code and then checks the condition, which guarantees that at least one iteration is carried out:\\n\\nLoops are widely used to process data in arrays and objects, or when an action has to be repeated until a particular state is reached.","for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {  \\n  console.log(i); \\n} \\n// Will print 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\n\\nlet i = 0; \\nwhile (i < 5) {  \\n  console.log(i);  \\n  i++; \\n}\\n\\nlet i = 0; \\ndo {  \\n  console.log(i);  \\n  i++; \\n} \\nwhile (i < 5);"],[626,"The `switch` construction is used to compare a single value with several possible variants (case) and to carry out particular code for each of them. The main keywords: `switch` — defines the construction, `case` — describes the possible variants, `break` — interrupts the carrying out of further checks, and `default` — states what to do if none of the conditions has been met.","The `switch` construction is an alternative to a multitude of `if...else` statements, when you have to check one and the same expression against several possible values. Its syntax looks like this:\\n\\nIn this example `day` is compared with the various values `1`, `2` and `3`. When a match is found, the corresponding block of code is carried out. The `break` keyword stops the execution after the first match, preventing the other blocks from being carried out. If no `case` fits, the `default` block is carried out.\\nThe keywords:\\n\\n- `switch`: initialises the construction.\\n\\n- `case`: sets a possible value to check against.\\n\\n- `break`: ends the execution of the current `case` block.\\n\\n- `default`: is carried out if none of the conditions has fitted.","let day = 3; \\nswitch (day) {  \\n  case 1:    \\n    console.log(\\"Monday\\");    \\n    break;  \\n  case 2:    \\n    console.log(\\"Tuesday\\");    \\n    break;  \\n  case 3:    \\n    console.log(\\"Wednesday\\");    \\n    break;  \\n  default:    \\n    console.log(\\"Invalid day\\"); }"],[627,"In JavaScript functions can be declared in several ways: through a function declaration, a function expression, arrow functions, and methods in objects. Each of these ways has its own peculiarities: function declarations are subject to \\"hoisting\\", function expressions are not, and arrow functions have no `this` context of their own.","In JavaScript there are several ways of declaring functions, and each has its own peculiarities:\\n\\n- Function Declaration: Declared using the `function` keyword and the name of the function. These functions are hoisted — that is, they can be called before they are defined in the code.\\n\\n- Function Expression: The function is saved into a variable. Unlike a function declaration, it is not hoisted.\\n\\n- Arrow Function: A short form of writing functions. The peculiarity is the absence of its own `this` context, which is convenient in callbacks and in functional components in React.\\n\\n- The methods of objects: Functions declared inside objects. They can access the object\'s properties through `this`.\\n\\nEach method of declaring is useful in different situations: for simplified code, for more convenient work with the context, or for structuring a program.","function greet() {  \\n  console.log(\\"Hello\\")\\n} \\ngreet(); // \\"Hello\\"\\n\\nconst greet = function() {  \\n  console.log(\\"Hello\\")\\n}\\ngreet(); // \\"Hello\\"\\n\\nconst greet = () => console.log(\\"Hello\\")\\ngreet() // \\"Hello\\"\\n\\nconst person = {  \\n  name: \\"John\\",  \\n  greet() {    \\n    console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}`) \\n  } \\n}\\nperson.greet(); // \\"Hello, John\\""],[628,"In JavaScript any values can be passed into a function: primitive types (numbers, strings, boolean values), objects, arrays, other functions (callbacks). You can also set default values for parameters, in case the function is called without them.","In JavaScript a function can take any data as arguments: numbers, strings, boolean values, objects, arrays, functions and even other functions as callbacks.\\n\\n- Primitive types: Passing numbers, strings, boolean values:\\n\\n- Objects and arrays: A function can take complex data structures, such as objects or arrays, which lets you pass more complex parameters:\\n\\n- Functions (callbacks): Other functions are often passed into a function as arguments in order to carry out operations after it has finished:\\n\\n- Default parameters: You can set default values for parameters in case they are not passed on the call:\\n\\nThis flexible approach lets you create universal functions that can work with any data and improves their reusability.","function add(a, b) {  \\n  return a + b; \\n} \\nconsole.log(add(5, 10)); // 15\\n\\nfunction greetUser(user) {  \\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}`); \\n} \\ngreetUser({ name: \\"John\\" }); // \\"Hello, John\\"\\n\\nfunction executeCallback(callback) {  \\n  callback(); \\n} \\nexecuteCallback(() => console.log(\\"Callback executed!\\")); // \\"Callback executed!\\"\\n\\nfunction greet(name = \\"Guest\\") {  \\n  console.log(`Hello, ${name}`); \\n} \\ngreet(); // \\"Hello, Guest\\""],[629,"Arrow functions are a shortened way of writing functions, which has no `this` context of its own. They are most often used to simplify the syntax and do not support some of the features of ordinary functions, `arguments` and `this` for example. These functions are convenient for writing short code, but they cannot be used in situations where the context matters.","Arrow functions are a shortened syntax for creating functions, introduced in ES6 (ECMAScript 2015).\\nThe main peculiarity of arrow functions is that they have no `this` context of their own — they take it from the outer scope, which makes them useful in some situations, when working with callbacks for example.\\nAn example of an arrow function:\\n\\nThe main differences between arrow functions and ordinary ones:\\n\\n- The `this` context: Arrow functions have no `this` of their own and capture it from the surrounding scope.\\n\\n- The syntax: They are shorter and more convenient for one-line expressions.\\n\\n- The absence of `arguments`: Arrow functions have no `arguments` object, so you have to use `rest` parameters to obtain the list of arguments.\\n\\n- They cannot be used as a constructor: Arrow functions cannot be used with the `new` keyword.\\n\\nAn example with the context:","const add = (a, b) => a + b; \\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // 5\\n\\nfunction Person() {  \\n  this.age = 0;  \\n  setInterval(() => {    \\n    this.age++; // \'this\' is taken from the outer function    \\n    console.log(this.age);  \\n  }, 1000); \\n} \\nconst p = new Person();"],[630,"A closure is a function that \\"remembers\\" its scope even after the outer function in which it was declared has finished executing. Closures let you create functions with access to variables from an outer function, which is useful for working with private data and preserving state between calls of the function.","A closure is the mechanism by which an inner function has access to the variables of an outer function, even if the outer function has already finished executing. Closures are created automatically in JavaScript when one function is returned or used inside another. That lets the inner function \\"remember\\" the variables from the outer scope.\\nAn example of a closure:\\n\\nHere `createCounter` returns an inner function, which preserves access to the `count` variable even after the outer function has finished executing. Closures thus let you preserve and manage state between calls of a function.\\nClosures are useful in various scenarios, for creating private variables for example, or for preserving state that should be available only through particular functions.","function createCounter() {  \\n  let count = 0;  \\n  return function() {    \\n    count++;    \\n    return count;  \\n  }; \\n} \\nconst counter = createCounter(); \\nconsole.log(counter()); // 1 \\nconsole.log(counter()); // 2"],[631,"In JavaScript there is one number type — `number`, which includes both whole numbers and floating-point numbers. There is also a special type, `BigInt`, which lets you work with very large whole numbers. In addition there are special numeric values, such as `NaN` (not a number) and `Infinity`.","In JavaScript there are two main types for working with numbers:\\n\\n- `number`: This is the main type for representing numbers in JavaScript. It includes both whole numbers and floating-point numbers:\\n\\nIt matters to note that JavaScript uses a 64-bit representation for numbers, which can sometimes lead to losses of precision when working with very large numbers or with floating-point numbers.\\n\\n- `BigInt`: This data type was added in later versions of JavaScript (ES2020) and lets you work with very large whole numbers that go beyond the safe values for the `number` type.\\n\\nSpecial numeric values:\\n\\n- `NaN` (Not-a-Number): This value is returned when the result of a mathematical operation is not a number — dividing a string by a number, for example.\\n\\n- `Infinity` and `-Infinity`: Represent positive and negative infinity respectively. They arise when dividing by zero.\\n\\nThe peculiarities of numbers in JavaScript:\\nFloating-point numbers can be imprecise, which matters to take into account during computations:\\n\\nAn understanding of these peculiarities helps you avoid errors when working with numeric values in JavaScript.","let integer = 42; // a whole number \\nlet float = 3.14; // a floating-point number\\n\\nlet bigInt = 9007199254740991n;\\n\\nlet result = \\"abc\\" / 2; // NaN\\n\\nlet positiveInfinity = 1 / 0; // Infinity \\nlet negativeInfinity = -1 / 0; // -Infinity\\n\\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2); // 0.30000000000000004"],[632,"In JavaScript there are a multitude of methods for working with strings, such as `charAt()`, `indexOf()`, `slice()`, `substring()`, `replace()`, `toUpperCase()`, `toLowerCase()`, `trim()`, `split()` and others. These methods let you find characters, change the case, divide a string into parts and carry out other operations.","Strings in JavaScript are a primitive data type, for which a multitude of methods are available. Here are several popular methods:\\n\\n- `charAt(index)`: Returns the character of the string at the position given.\\n\\n- `indexOf(substring)`: Returns the index of the first occurrence of a substring in the string. If the substring is not found, it returns `-1`.\\n\\n- `slice(start, end)`: Returns the part of the string beginning at `start` and ending before `end`.\\n\\n- `toUpperCase()` and `toLowerCase()`: Convert a string into upper or lower case.\\n\\n- `trim()`: Removes the spaces at the beginning and the end of a string.\\n\\n- `split(delimiter)`: Divides a string into an array of parts by the delimiter given.\\n\\nA knowledge of these methods helps you work with strings effectively and carry out textual transformations.","let str = \\"Hello\\"; \\nconsole.log(str.charAt(1)); // \\"e\\"\\n\\nlet str = \\"Hello World\\"; \\nconsole.log(str.indexOf(\\"World\\")); // 6\\n\\nlet str = \\"Hello\\"; \\nconsole.log(str.slice(1, 4)); // \\"ell\\"\\n\\nlet str = \\"Hello\\"; \\nconsole.log(str.toUpperCase()); // \\"HELLO\\"\\n\\nlet str = \\"  Hello  \\"; \\nconsole.log(str.trim()); // \\"Hello\\"\\n\\nlet str = \\"Hello World\\"; \\nconsole.log(str.split(\\" \\")); // [\\"Hello\\", \\"World\\"]"],[633,"The number methods in JavaScript include `toFixed()`, `toPrecision()`, `parseInt()`, `parseFloat()`, `Math.round()`, `Math.floor()`, `Math.ceil()`, `Math.random()` and others. They are used for rounding, generating random numbers and converting strings into numbers.","Numbers in JavaScript can be processed by means of a multitude of built-in methods and functions:\\n\\n- `toFixed(n)`: Rounds a number to `n` digits after the decimal point and returns a string.\\n\\n- `toPrecision(n)`: Returns the number with the given total quantity of significant digits.\\n\\n- `parseInt(string)` and `parseFloat(string)`: Convert a string into a whole or a fractional number respectively.\\n\\n- `Math.round()`: Rounds a number to the nearest whole number.\\n\\n- `Math.floor()`: Rounds a number down to the nearest whole number.\\n\\n- `Math.ceil()`: Rounds a number up to the nearest whole number.\\n\\n- `Math.random()`: Returns a random number in the range from 0 (inclusive) to 1 (exclusive).\\n\\nThese methods let you carry out arithmetic operations, rounding, the generation of random numbers and conversions between strings and numbers.","let num = 3.14159; \\nconsole.log(num.toFixed(2)); // \\"3.14\\"\\n\\nlet num = 123.456; \\nconsole.log(num.toPrecision(4)); // \\"123.5\\"\\n\\nconsole.log(parseInt(\\"123px\\")); // 123 \\nconsole.log(parseFloat(\\"123.45px\\")); // 123.45\\n\\nconsole.log(Math.round(2.5)); // 3\\n\\nconsole.log(Math.floor(2.9)); // 2\\n\\nconsole.log(Math.ceil(2.1)); // 3\\n\\nconsole.log(Math.random()); // For example, 0.123456"],[634,"An object is a data structure that holds information in the form of \\"key-value\\" pairs. The keys are usually strings (or symbols), and the values can be of any type, including other objects. Objects are used to hold related data and to work with it as a single whole.","Objects in JavaScript are a fundamental data structure, which lets you hold data in the form of \\"key-value\\" pairs. An object\'s keys must be strings or symbols, and the values can be anything: numbers, strings, arrays, functions and even other objects.\\nAn example of an object:\\n\\nObjects are useful when data has to be grouped together. In the `person` object, for example, you can hold information about a person: their name, their age and a method for greeting. Thanks to objects the code becomes more structured and organised.\\nObjects also play a key role in prototypal inheritance. Every object can inherit properties and methods from another object through prototypes, which makes them a powerful tool for creating complex data structures and models of behaviour.","let person = {  \\n  name: \\"John\\",  \\n  age: 30,  \\n  greet: function() {    \\n    console.log(\\"Hello, \\" + this.name);  \\n  } \\n}; \\nconsole.log(person.name); // \\"John\\" \\nperson.greet(); // \\"Hello, John\\""],[635,"Objects in JavaScript are stored by reference. That means that when an object is assigned to a variable or passed into a function, what is copied is not the object itself but the reference to it. Any changes to the object through one variable will therefore also be visible through the other variables that refer to that object.","In JavaScript objects are stored by reference, unlike the primitive data types (numbers and strings, for example), which are copied by value. When you create an object and assign it to a variable, the variable holds not the object itself but a reference to the place in memory where that object is located. It matters to understand this, since if several variables point to one and the same object, changes brought in through one variable will be reflected in the other variables that refer to the same object.\\n\\nThis behaviour has to be taken into account in order to avoid undesirable side effects, especially when working with objects passed into functions or returned from them.","let obj1 = { name: \\"John\\" }; \\nlet obj2 = obj1; \\nobj2.name = \\"Jane\\"; \\nconsole.log(obj1.name); // \\"Jane\\", since obj1 and obj2 refer to one object."],[636,"In JavaScript objects are compared by reference rather than by value. That means two objects are considered equal (`===`) only if they refer to one and the same object in memory. Even if two objects have identical properties and values, they will not be equal if they are different instances.","In JavaScript, when objects are compared a check of the references to one and the same object is used rather than of their content. That differs from the primitive types, which are compared by value. When you compare two objects with the `===` or `==` operator, JavaScript checks whether they point to one and the same place in memory. If they do, the objects are considered equal; if not, they are considered different, even if their properties are identical.\\n\\nIf you have to compare objects by their content, you have to implement a special function that will check every property of the objects.\\nAn example of a deep comparison function:","let obj1 = { name: \\"John\\" }; \\nlet obj2 = { name: \\"John\\" }; \\nlet obj3 = obj1; \\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj2); // false, different objects, although with identical data \\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj3); // true, both refer to one object\\n\\nfunction deepEqual(obj1, obj2) {  \\n  return JSON.stringify(obj1) === JSON.stringify(obj2); \\n}"],[637,"Objects can be copied in several ways. A shallow copy can be made by means of `Object.assign()` or the spread operator `{ ...obj }`, while for a deep copy `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` is often used. A shallow copy copies only the top level of the properties, whereas a deep copy copies every level of the object, including the nested ones.","In JavaScript there are several ways of copying objects, and they divide into shallow and deep copies.\\n\\n- Shallow copies:\\n\\n- `Object.assign()`: Creates a new copy of an object, copying only the properties of the top level. Nested objects are copied by reference.\\n\\n- The spread operator (`...`): Works in the same way as `Object.assign()`, creating a shallow copy of the object.\\n\\n- Deep copies:\\n\\n- `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())`: A simple way of creating a deep copy; it does not work with functions and other special objects (`Date`, `Map`, `Set`, for example), however.\\n\\n- A recursive function: To create a deep copy that works correctly with complex structures, you can use recursive functions or special libraries of the `Lodash` kind.\\n\\nFor everyday work shallow copies are often enough, but if an object contains nested structures it is better to use deep copying, in order to avoid problems with data changing by reference.","let original = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } }; \\nlet copy = Object.assign({}, original); \\ncopy.b.c = 3; \\nconsole.log(original.b.c); // 3, since the nested object was copied by reference.\\n\\nlet original = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } }; \\nlet copy = { ...original };\\n\\nlet original = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } }; \\nlet copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original)); \\ncopy.b.c = 3; \\nconsole.log(original.b.c); // 2, since the object was fully copied.\\n\\nfunction deepCopy(obj) {  \\nreturn Array.isArray(obj)\\n    ? obj.map(deepCopy)\\n    : typeof obj === \'object\'\\n    ? Object.keys(obj).reduce((acc, key) => {\\n        acc[key] = deepCopy(obj[key]);\\n        return acc;\\n      }, {})\\n    : obj;\\n}"],[638,"The main methods of objects include `Object.keys()`, which returns an array of the object\'s keys, `Object.values()`, which returns an array of the object\'s values, and `Object.entries()`, which returns an array of key-value pairs. There is also `Object.assign()`, which is used to copy objects, and `Object.freeze()`, which \\"freezes\\" an object, preventing it from being changed any further.","In JavaScript there are several built-in methods for working with objects:\\n\\n- `Object.keys(obj)`: Returns an array of the object\'s keys.\\n\\n- `Object.values(obj)`: Returns an array of the object\'s values.\\n\\n- `Object.entries(obj)`: Returns an array of arrays, where each pair represents a key and its value.\\n\\n- `Object.assign(target, ...sources)`: Copies the properties from one or several source objects into a target object.\\n\\n- `Object.freeze(obj)`: \\"Freezes\\" an object, making it immutable.\\n\\nThese methods make working with objects simpler, providing the means for obtaining, copying and protecting the data in objects.","const obj = { name: \'John\', age: 30 }; \\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj)); // [\'name\', \'age\']\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.values(obj)); // [\'John\', 30]\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.entries(obj)); // [[\'name\', \'John\'], [\'age\', 30]]\\n\\nconst target = { a: 1 }; \\nconst source = { b: 2, c: 3 }; \\nObject.assign(target, source); \\nconsole.log(target); // { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 }\\n\\nconst frozenObj = Object.freeze({ name: \'John\' }); \\nfrozenObj.name = \'Doe\'; // Error: the object is frozen"],[639,"In the methods of an object `this` points to the object itself to which the method belongs. That lets an object\'s methods work with its properties and other methods. The value of `this` can change depending on how the method is called, so it matters to understand the context of the call.","In JavaScript `this` is a keyword that points to the current execution context. In the methods of an object `this` usually refers to the object itself, letting the methods interact with its properties. That is convenient for structuring code and organising the logic inside objects.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example `this.name` refers to the `name` property of the `person` object. The value of `this` depends on the context of the call, however. If the method is called outside the object or passed into another function, for example, the `this` context can be lost:\\n\\nTo preserve the `this` context you can use methods of the `.bind()` or `.call()` kind, or arrow functions, which do not create their own `this` context.\\nAn example with `.bind()`:\\n\\nThe correct use of `this` thus matters for correct access to an object\'s data inside its methods.","const person = {  \\n  name: \'John\',  \\n  greet() {    \\n    console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name}`);  \\n  } \\n}; \\nperson.greet(); // Hello, my name is John\\n\\nconst greet = person.greet; \\ngreet(); // undefined, since `this` now points to the global object\\n\\nconst boundGreet = person.greet.bind(person); \\nboundGreet(); // Hello, my name is John"],[640,"An object constructor is used to create new instances of objects with an identical structure and methods. In JavaScript you can use a constructor function or a class for that. The constructor must be called with the `new` keyword, which creates a new object, linking it with the constructor\'s prototype.","An object constructor is a function or a class used to create new objects with identical properties and methods. In JavaScript you can use both constructor functions and the class syntax to create objects. In both cases the constructor initialises the new object and sets its properties.\\nAn example of a constructor function:\\n\\nHere `Person` is the constructor, and `john` is a new instance of the `Person` object. It matters to use `new` when calling the constructor, in order to create a new object and link it with the constructor\'s prototype.\\nStarting with ECMAScript 2015 (ES6), you can also use classes to create objects:\\n\\nClasses make the syntax of creating objects more convenient and structured, but under the hood they work in the same way as constructor functions.\\nThe rules of working with constructors:\\n\\n- The constructor must be called with `new`.\\n\\n- `this` inside the constructor refers to the new object.\\n\\n- You can add methods through the prototype for logic common to all of the objects.\\n\\nConstructors are used to create a multitude of objects with an identical structure, which makes the code more organised and effective.","function Person(name, age) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.age = age;\\n} \\nconst john = new Person(\'John\', 30); \\nconsole.log(john.name); // John\\n\\nclass Person {\\n  constructor(name, age) {\\n    this.name = name;    \\n    this.age = age;\\n  }\\n}\\nconst jane = new Person(\'Jane\', 25); \\nconsole.log(jane.name); // Jane"],[641,"The flags of an object\'s properties in JavaScript determine how the properties can be changed, deleted or overwritten. The main flags are `writable`, `enumerable` and `configurable`. They can be changed by means of the `Object.defineProperty()` method, which lets you set or change these characteristics for a specific property of an object.","In JavaScript the properties of objects can have several flags, which control their behaviour:\\n\\n- `writable`: If this flag is set to `true`, the value of the property can be changed. If it is set to `false`, an attempt to change the value of the property will not lead to an error, but it will not change it.\\n\\n- `enumerable`: This flag determines whether the property will be displayed when going through the object with loops such as `for...in` or when `Object.keys()` is called. If the flag is set to `false`, the property will not be enumerated.\\n\\n- `configurable`: If this flag is set to `true`, the property can be deleted and its flags changed (it can be made non-writable, for example). If it is set to `false`, the property cannot be deleted, and its flags cannot be changed.\\n\\nTo change the flags of properties the `Object.defineProperty()` method is used:\\n\\nIn this example the `prop` property cannot be changed because of the `writable: false` flag.\\nBy changing the flags of properties, developers can control how the properties of objects can interact with the code, which helps maintain the integrity of the data.","const obj = {};\\nObject.defineProperty(obj, \'prop\', {\\n  value: 42,\\n  writable: false,\\n  enumerable: true,\\n  configurable: true, \\n});\\nobj.prop = 33; // Will not change \\nconsole.log(obj.prop); // 42"],[642,"To seal an object in JavaScript means to make it immutable, so that its properties cannot be added, deleted or changed. That can be done by means of the `Object.freeze()` method, which freezes the object and makes its properties immutable.","Sealing an object in JavaScript is the process that prevents changes to an object after it has been created. That is achieved by means of the `Object.freeze()` method, which makes all of the object\'s properties immutable.\\nWhen an object is sealed, you cannot add new properties, delete existing ones or change their values. Attempts to change a sealed object will not raise errors, but the changes will not be applied.\\nAn example of the use of `Object.freeze()`:\\n\\nIf you want the properties to be sealed recursively (so that the nested objects also become immutable), you will have to write a function that will call `Object.freeze()` on every nested object.\\nSealing objects is useful when the state of the data has to be protected and accidental changes prevented, which is especially important in large applications and libraries.","const obj = {\\n  name: \'John\',  age: 30 \\n};\\nObject.freeze(obj); // An attempt to change it\\nobj.age = 31; // Will not change\\nobj.city = \'New York\'; // Will not be added\\ndelete obj.name; // Will not be deleted\\nconsole.log(obj); // { name: \'John\', age: 30 }"],[643,"Getters and setters are special methods that let you control access to an object\'s properties. Getters let you obtain the values of properties, while setters let you establish their values. They make working with objects simpler by providing an interface for working with the data.","Getters and setters in JavaScript are methods that let you control access to an object\'s properties. They let you wrap properties and manage how the data is read and written.\\n\\n- Getters: These are methods that return the value of a property. They are defined by means of the `get` keyword before the name of the method.\\n\\n- Setters: These are methods that let you establish the values of properties. They are defined by means of the `set` keyword before the name of the method.\\n\\nGetters and setters let you encapsulate the logic of accessing the data, which helps avoid direct interaction with an object\'s internal properties. That also lets you check the data before establishing it and carry out additional operations — logging or validation, for example.\\nGetters and setters can be used with `Object.defineProperty()`, in order to state their behaviour more explicitly, especially when the flags for the properties have to be configured.\\nGetters and setters thus provide a cleaner and more manageable way of working with the data inside objects, which makes the code more readable and safer.","const person = {\\n  firstName: \'John\',\\n  lastName: \'Doe\',\\n  get fullName() {\\n    return `${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`;\\n  }\\n};\\nconsole.log(person.fullName); // John Doe\\n\\nconst person = {\\n  firstName: \'John\',\\n  lastName: \'Doe\',\\n  set fullName(name) {\\n    const parts = name.split(\' \');\\n    this.firstName = parts[0];\\n    this.lastName = parts[1];\\n  } \\n};\\nperson.fullName = \'Jane Smith\';\\nconsole.log(person.firstName); // Jane\\nconsole.log(person.lastName); // Smith"],[644,"An array is an ordered collection of values, which can contain elements of any type, including numbers, strings, objects and even other arrays. In JavaScript arrays are objects and can be mutable, which lets you add, delete and change elements at any time. That makes arrays a convenient tool for holding and working with a set of data.","Arrays in JavaScript are a special type of object, which lets you hold several values in a single variable. They are ordered collections, where every element has its own index, starting from zero. That means you can easily gain access to an array\'s elements using their index.\\nAn example of creating an array:\\n\\nThe data type of an array in JavaScript is an object, and they can hold values of various types. That lets you create arrays containing strings, numbers and even other arrays:\\n\\nArrays are flexible and powerful tools that can be used to hold and process data — in loops and in methods such as `map`, `filter` and `reduce`, for example — which makes them a key element in JavaScript development.","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconsole.log(fruits[0]); // apple\\n\\nconst mixedArray = [1, \'two\', { name: \'three\' }, [4]]; \\nconsole.log(mixedArray[2].name); // three"],[645,"In JavaScript, to delete an element from an array you can use several methods, such as `pop()`, `shift()` and `splice()`. The `pop()` method deletes the last element of the array, `shift()` deletes the first element, and `splice()` lets you delete elements at a given index and can delete several elements at once. Each of these methods changes the original array.","Deleting elements from an array in JavaScript can be carried out in several ways, depending on which element has to be deleted and on its position:\\n\\n- `pop()`: This method deletes the last element of the array and returns it. If the array is empty, `pop()` will return `undefined`.\\n\\n- `shift()`: This method deletes the first element of the array and returns it. If the array is empty, `shift()` will return `undefined`.\\n\\n- `splice()`: This method lets you delete elements by index. It takes two arguments: the index of the element that has to be deleted, and the number of elements to delete.\\n\\n- Besides that, you can create a new array excluding the elements you do not need, using the `filter()` method:\\n\\nThe methods for deleting elements in arrays give developers the ability to manage the content flexibly, which matters for working with data effectively.","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst lastFruit = fruits.pop(); \\nconsole.log(fruits); // [\'apple\', \'banana\'] \\nconsole.log(lastFruit); // orange\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\'];\\nconst firstFruit = fruits.shift(); \\nconsole.log(fruits); // [\'banana\', \'orange\'] \\nconsole.log(firstFruit); // apple\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfruits.splice(1, 1); // We delete the element with index 1 \\nconsole.log(fruits); // [\'apple\', \'orange\']\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst newFruits = fruits.filter(fruit => fruit !== \'banana\'); \\nconsole.log(newFruits); // [\'apple\', \'orange\']"],[646,"In JavaScript arrays can be created in several ways: by means of array literals, the `Array` constructor, the `Array.of()` method and the `Array.from()` method. Array literals are the simplest and most widespread way, while the constructor lets you create arrays with a given length or initialised with values.","There are several ways of creating arrays in JavaScript, and each of them has its own peculiarities:\\n\\n- The literal syntax: This is the simplest and most widespread way. Square brackets are used to declare the array and enumerate its elements.\\n\\n- The `Array` constructor: You can create an array by means of the constructor. You can pass it the length of the array or the elements for initialisation.\\n\\n- The `Array.of()` method: This method creates a new array with the elements given. It takes any number of arguments and creates an array with those elements.\\n\\n- The `Array.from()` method: This method lets you create an array from array-like objects or iterable objects. That is useful if you want to convert strings or a NodeList into an array, for example.\\n\\nEach of these ways has its own advantages depending on the context of use. Array literals are the most convenient for creating arrays quickly, for example, whereas the `Array.of()` and `Array.from()` methods provide flexibility when creating arrays from other sources of data.","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']\\n\\nconst numbers = new Array(3); // Creates an array with the length 3\\nconst fruits = new Array(\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\'); // Creates an array with the elements\\n\\nconst fruits = Array.of(\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\');\\n\\nconst str = \'hello\';\\nconst charsArray = Array.from(str); // [\'h\', \'e\', \'l\', \'l\', \'o\']"],[647,"In JavaScript there are several ways of iterating over arrays, including the `for` loop, `forEach`, `map`, `filter` and `reduce`. The `for` loop and `forEach` are convenient for carrying out actions with every element of an array, whereas `map` and `filter` create new arrays on the basis of existing ones. The `reduce` method lets you accumulate values into one — for counting a sum, for example.","Iterating over arrays in JavaScript can be carried out in various ways, depending on the tasks that have to be solved. Here are several of the main methods:\\n\\n- The `for` loop: This is one of the most traditional ways of iterating over an array, which lets you gain access to every element by index.\\n\\n- The `forEach()` method: This method carries out a given function for every element of the array. It does not return a new array but simply goes through all of the elements.\\n\\n- The `map()` method: This method creates a new array, where every element is the result of carrying out a given function for every element of the original array. It suits cases where the data has to be transformed.\\n\\n- The `filter()` method: This method returns a new array consisting only of the elements that satisfy a given condition.\\n\\n- The `reduce()` method: This method lets you accumulate the values of an array into one, using a given function. That can be useful for computing sums, averages and so on.\\n\\nEach of these methods has its own peculiarities and suits different tasks. `forEach` and `map` suit working with elements without the need to change the original array, for example, whereas `reduce` lets you combine data into a single value.","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfor (let i = 0; i < fruits.length; i++) {\\n  console.log(fruits[i]);\\n}\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfruits.forEach(fruit => {    \\n  console.log(fruit); \\n});\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3]; \\nconst doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2); // [2, 4, 6]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]; \\nconst evens = numbers.filter(num => num % 2 === 0); // [2, 4]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((acc, num) => acc + num, 0); // 10"],[648,"The main methods of arrays in JavaScript include `push()`, `pop()`, `shift()`, `unshift()`, `splice()`, `slice()`, `forEach()`, `map()`, `filter()`, `reduce()`, `find()`, `includes()` and `join()`. These methods let you add, delete, change, iterate over and check the elements of an array, which makes them indispensable for working with collections of data.","JavaScript offers a multitude of methods for working with arrays, each of which carries out its own task. Here are some of the main methods:\\n\\n- `push()`: Adds one or several elements to the end of the array and returns the array\'s new length.\\n\\n- `pop()`: Deletes the last element of the array and returns it.\\n\\n- `shift()`: Deletes the first element of the array and returns it.\\n\\n- `unshift()`: Adds one or several elements to the beginning of the array and returns the array\'s new length.\\n\\n- `splice()`: Deletes or replaces the elements of an array at a given index. Lets you delete several elements.\\n\\n- `slice()`: Returns a new array containing a copy of part of the original array. Does not change the original array.\\n\\n- `forEach()`: Carries out a given function for every element of the array.\\n\\n- `map()`: Creates a new array containing the results of calling a function for every element.\\n\\n- `filter()`: Creates a new array consisting only of the elements that satisfy a condition.\\n\\n- `reduce()`: Applies a function to every element of the array in order to accumulate a single result.\\n\\n- `find()`: Returns the first element of the array that satisfies a condition.\\n\\n- `includes()`: Checks whether the element given is contained in the array, returning `true` or `false`.\\n\\n- `join()`: Joins all of the elements of the array into a string, using the delimiter given.\\n\\nThese methods make arrays in JavaScript powerful tools for working with data, providing flexibility and simplicity in manipulating collections.","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\']; \\nfruits.push(\'orange\'); // [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst lastFruit = fruits.pop(); // orange\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst firstFruit = fruits.shift(); // apple\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfruits.unshift(\'apple\'); // [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfruits.splice(1, 1); // Deletes \'banana\'\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst citrus = fruits.slice(1); // [\'banana\', \'orange\']\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfruits.forEach(fruit => console.log(fruit));\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3]; \\nconst doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2); // [2, 4, 6]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst evens = numbers.filter(num => num % 2 === 0); // [2, 4]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((acc, num) => acc + num, 0); // 10\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst found = numbers.find(num => num > 2); // 3\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst hasBanana = fruits.includes(\'banana\'); // true\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconst result = fruits.join(\', \'); // \'apple, banana, orange\'"],[649,"In JavaScript you can use the `Array.isArray()` method to check whether a variable is an array. This method returns `true` if the argument passed is an array, and `false` otherwise. You can also use the `instanceof` operator, but it is less preferable, since it may not work in some cases with different execution contexts.","In JavaScript the check as to whether a variable is an array can be carried out by means of several approaches, but the most reliable way is the use of the `Array.isArray()` method.\\nThe `Array.isArray()` method: This method takes one argument and returns `true` if that argument is an array, and `false` otherwise.\\n\\nThe `instanceof` operator: This operator checks whether an object is an instance of a particular class. It can be used to check for an array, but it is less reliable, especially in situations with several execution contexts (if the array was created in another frame or window, for example).\\n\\nAlthough `instanceof` can work in most cases, it is recommended to use `Array.isArray()` as a more reliable and understandable method for checking whether an element is an array","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(fruits)); // true \\n\\nconst notArray = { name: \'apple\' }; \\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(notArray)); // false\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nconsole.log(fruits instanceof Array); // true"],[650,"Mutating methods change the original array or object, whereas non-mutating methods return a new array or object without changing the original. The `push()` and `pop()` methods, for example, are mutating, because they change the original array. Whereas `map()` and `filter()` are non-mutating, since they create a new array on the basis of the original.","In JavaScript the methods for working with arrays and objects divide into mutating and non-mutating ones.\\nMutating methods: These methods change the original array or object. For example:\\n\\n- `push()`: Adds one or several elements to the end of the array and changes it.\\n\\n- `splice()`: Lets you delete or replace elements in an array, changing it.\\n\\nNon-mutating methods: These methods return a new array or object without changing the original. For example:\\n\\n- `map()`: Creates a new array, where every element is the result of carrying out a given function for every element of the original array.\\n\\n- `filter()`: Creates a new array consisting only of the elements that satisfy a given condition.\\n\\nAn understanding of the differences between mutating and non-mutating methods matters, since it can affect the performance and the predictability of the code. When using libraries for managing state, such as Redux, for example, it is preferable to use non-mutating methods, in order to avoid unpredictable changes of the state.","const fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\']; \\nfruits.push(\'orange\'); // [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']\\n\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\']; \\nfruits.splice(1, 1); // Deletes \'banana\'\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3]; \\nconst doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2); // [2, 4, 6]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]; \\nconst evens = numbers.filter(num => num % 2 === 0); // [2, 4]"],[651,"Destructuring is a convenient way of extracting values from arrays and objects and assigning them to variables. By means of destructuring you can work with data quickly and easily, extracting the values you need in a few lines of code. That makes the code more readable and makes working with complex data structures simpler.","Destructuring in JavaScript is a syntactic construction that lets you conveniently extract values from arrays and objects and assign them to variables. That makes working with data simpler and makes the code cleaner and more understandable.\\n\\n- The destructuring of arrays: Lets you extract the elements of an array into variables. For example:\\n\\n- The destructuring of objects: Lets you extract the properties of an object into variables. For example:\\n\\n- The parameters of a function: Destructuring can also be used in the parameters of functions in order to extract values from objects:\\n\\nDestructuring is especially useful when working with functions that take objects or arrays as arguments, since it lets you extract at once only those values you need. That makes the code simpler and improves its readability.","const colors = [\'red\', \'green\', \'blue\']; \\nconst [firstColor, secondColor] = colors; // firstColor = \'red\', secondColor = \'green\'\\n\\nconst person = { name: \'Alice\', age: 25 }; \\nconst { name, age } = person; // name = \'Alice\', age = 25\\n\\nfunction greet({ name }) {    \\n  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`); \\n} \\ngreet({ name: \'Alice\' }); // Hello, Alice!"],[652,"The rest operator (`...`) is used in JavaScript to gather the remaining arguments into an array when defining a function. It lets you pass a variable number of arguments and work with them easily, without worrying about how many arguments will be passed. That is especially convenient when a multitude of values have to be processed.","The rest operator in JavaScript is denoted as `...` and is used to gather the remaining arguments into an array when a function is called with a variable number of arguments. That lets developers work easily with the multitude of values passed into a function.\\n\\n- An example of use in a function: The rest operator can be used in the declaration of a function to gather all of the arguments that were not explicitly stated.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n- Combining with other parameters: The rest operator can be used together with other parameters, but it must be placed at the end of the list of parameters.\\n\\n- Use with arrays and objects: The rest operator can also be used to gather the remaining properties of an object in destructuring.\\n\\nThe rest operator makes working with functions more flexible and makes passing a multitude of arguments simpler, as well as manipulations with objects and arrays. That is especially useful in situations where the quantity of data being passed is not known in advance.","function sum(...numbers) {\\n  return numbers.reduce((acc, num) => acc + num, 0); \\n} \\nconsole.log(sum(1, 2, 3, 4)); // 10\\n\\nfunction printInfo(name, ...info) {\\n  console.log(`Name: ${name}`);    \\n  console.log(`Info: ${info.join(\', \')}`); \\n} \\nprintInfo(\'Alice\', 25, \'Engineer\', \'New York\'); \\n// Name: Alice \\n// Info: 25, Engineer, New York\\n\\nconst person = { name: \'Alice\', age: 25, city: \'New York\' }; \\nconst { name, ...otherInfo } = person; \\nconsole.log(otherInfo); // { age: 25, city: \'New York\' }"],[653,"The spread operator (`...`) lets you easily copy and combine arrays and objects by means of a more concise and readable syntax. Unlike the `concat()` method, which requires the array to be combined to be stated explicitly, and `Object.assign()`, which can make copying objects with nested structures more difficult, the spread operator makes these operations intuitively understandable and less prone to errors. That makes writing code simpler and raises its readability.","The spread operator in JavaScript, denoted as `...`, provides a powerful and convenient way of working with arrays and objects, letting you easily copy, combine and change them. Let us look at the advantages compared with the traditional methods.\\nCombining arrays:\\n\\n- By means of the spread operator:\\n\\n- By means of `concat()`:\\n\\nThe spread operator lets you not only combine arrays but also insert elements at arbitrary positions.\\nCopying objects:\\n\\n- By means of the spread operator:\\n\\n- By means of `Object.assign()`:\\n\\nThe spread operator does not require an empty object to be stated explicitly in order to create a copy, which makes the process simpler. It also creates more predictable behaviour when copying nested objects, since there is no risk of nested objects changing as there is when using `Object.assign()`.\\nFlexibility and readability: The spread operator improves the readability of the code, since it lets you use a simple notation intuitively. That is especially useful in situations where a new array or object has to be created on the basis of an existing one, adding or changing some properties.\\nIn conclusion, the spread operator makes working with arrays and objects substantially simpler, providing a cleaner and more understandable syntax compared with the traditional methods such as `concat()` and `Object.assign()`.","const array1 = [1, 2, 3]; \\nconst array2 = [4, 5, 6]; \\nconst combined = [...array1, ...array2]; // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\\n\\nconst combined = array1.concat(array2); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\\n\\nconst obj1 = { a: 1, b: 2 }; \\nconst obj2 = { ...obj1, c: 3 }; // { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 }\\n\\nconst obj2 = Object.assign({}, obj1, { c: 3 }); // { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 }"],[654,"`Map` is a collection that holds key-value pairs and lets you use keys of any type, unlike ordinary objects. It has methods for adding, deleting and obtaining elements, such as `set()`, `get()`, `delete()` and `has()`. You can iterate over the elements in a `Map` by means of the `forEach()` method or with a `for...of` loop, which makes working with this data structure convenient and flexible.","`Map` in JavaScript is a built-in data structure intended for holding key-value pairs. It offers several peculiarities and advantages compared with ordinary objects:\\nKeys of any type: Unlike objects, where the keys can only be strings or symbols, `Map` lets you use any data types as keys, including functions, objects and even other maps.\\n\\nThe methods of `Map`:\\n\\n- `set(key, value)`: Adds or updates the element with the key given.\\n\\n- `get(key)`: Returns the value at the key given.\\n\\n- `has(key)`: Checks whether the key given exists.\\n\\n- `delete(key)`: Deletes the element at the key given.\\n\\n- `clear()`: Deletes all of the elements from the map.\\n\\n- `size`: A property returning the number of key-value pairs in the `Map`.\\n\\nIterating over the elements: `Map` provides several ways of iterating over its elements:\\n\\n- The `forEach()` method:\\n\\n- The `for...of` loop:\\n\\n- The `keys()`, `values()` and `entries()` methods:\\n\\n`Map` is an especially useful structure when it matters to preserve the order in which the elements were inserted, and also to ensure effective access by keys. That makes `Map` an excellent choice for implementing caches and other data structures where fast operations of searching, adding and deleting are required.","const myMap = new Map(); \\nmyMap.set({}, \'value\'); // The key is an object \\nmyMap.set(\'stringKey\', \'value\'); // The key is a string \\nmyMap.set(42, \'value\'); // The key is a number\\n\\nmyMap.forEach((value, key) => {\\n  console.log(`${key}: ${value}`); \\n});\\n\\nfor (const [key, value] of myMap) {\\n  console.log(`${key}: ${value}`); \\n}\\n\\nfor (const key of myMap.keys()) {\\n  console.log(key); \\n} \\nfor (const value of myMap.values()) {\\n  console.log(value); \\n} \\nfor (const [key, value] of myMap.entries()) {\\n  console.log(`${key}: ${value}`); \\n}"],[655,"`Set` is a collection of unique values, which deletes duplicates automatically. Every element in a `Set` is held only once, regardless of how many times it was added. `Set` has methods for adding, deleting and checking values, such as `add()`, `delete()`, `has()` and `clear()`. You can iterate over the elements in a `Set` by means of the `forEach()` method or a `for...of` loop.","`Set` in JavaScript is a built-in data structure intended for holding unique values. The main peculiarities and advantages of `Set` include:\\nUnique values: In a `Set` there can be no duplicates. If you attempt to add a value that already exists, it is simply ignored.\\n\\nThe methods of `Set`:\\n\\n- `add(value)`: Adds a value to the `Set`.\\n\\n- `delete(value)`: Deletes a value from the `Set`.\\n\\n- `has(value)`: Checks whether a value exists in the `Set`.\\n\\n- `clear()`: Deletes all of the values from the `Set`.\\n\\n- `size`: A property returning the number of unique values in the set.\\n\\nIterating over the elements: `Set` offers several ways of iterating over its elements:\\n\\n- The `forEach()` method:\\n\\n- The `for...of` loop:\\n\\n`Set` is a useful structure when a collection of unique values has to be held and operations of searching and deleting carried out effectively. That makes `Set` a good choice for tasks connected with filtering duplicates and tracking unique elements.","const mySet = new Set(); \\nmySet.add(1); \\nmySet.add(2); \\nmySet.add(1); // Ignored, since 1 is already in the set \\nconsole.log(mySet); // Set { 1, 2 }\\n\\nmySet.forEach(value => {\\n  console.log(value); \\n});\\n\\nfor (const value of mySet) {\\n  console.log(value); \\n}"],[656,"`WeakMap` is a collection of key-value pairs, where the keys can only be objects and the values can be of any type. The main peculiarity of `WeakMap` is that it does not hold strong references to the keys, which lets the garbage collector clean up the elements automatically if there are no longer any references to them. `WeakMap` has the methods `set()`, `get()`, `has()` and `delete()`, but it cannot be iterated over by means of the ordinary methods, since `WeakMap` provides no iterators.","`WeakMap` is a special data structure in JavaScript intended for holding key-value pairs, where the keys can only be objects and the values can be of any type. The peculiarities of `WeakMap` include:\\nWeak references: Unlike an ordinary `Map`, `WeakMap` does not hold strong references to its keys. That means that if there are no longer any references to the key object, the garbage collector can free the memory, freeing the corresponding value in the `WeakMap` too. That makes `WeakMap` useful for caching or for holding metadata without the risk of memory leaks.\\nThe methods of `WeakMap`:\\n\\n- `set(key, value)`: Adds or updates the value at the key given.\\n\\n- `get(key)`: Returns the value at the key given.\\n\\n- `has(key)`: Checks whether the key given exists.\\n\\n- `delete(key)`: Deletes the element at the key given.\\n\\nThe absence of iterators: Unfortunately `WeakMap` provides no methods for iterating over the elements, such as `forEach()`, `keys()`, `values()` or `entries()`. That is connected with the fact that `WeakMap` can contain only weak references and does not guarantee that every key will be available for iteration at any given moment in time.\\n`WeakMap` is thus ideally suited to cases where you have to hold temporary data connected with objects without affecting their life cycle. You can use `WeakMap` to hold cached data connected with DOM elements, for example, which lets you prevent memory leaks.",null],[657,"`WeakSet` is a collection of unique objects, where every object is held using a weak reference. The main peculiarity of `WeakSet` is that it does not hold references to its elements, which lets the garbage collector delete objects automatically if there are no longer any references to them. The methods of `WeakSet` include `add()`, `delete()` and `has()`, but it cannot be iterated over, since `WeakSet` provides no iterators.","`WeakSet` in JavaScript is a collection of unique objects, intended for holding values using weak references. The main peculiarities of `WeakSet`:\\n\\n- Unique values: Only unique objects can be held in a `WeakSet`. If you attempt to add an object that already exists in the `WeakSet`, it will simply be ignored.\\n\\n- Weak references: Since `WeakSet` uses weak references to its elements, if an object no longer has strong references it can be deleted by the garbage collector. That makes `WeakSet` useful for holding temporary data connected with objects, without the risk of memory leaks.\\n\\nThe methods of `WeakSet`:\\n\\n- `add(value)`: Adds an object to the `WeakSet`.\\n\\n- `delete(value)`: Deletes an object from the `WeakSet`.\\n\\n- `has(value)`: Checks whether an object exists in the `WeakSet`.\\n\\nThe absence of iterators: As in the case of `WeakMap`, `WeakSet` provides no methods for iterating over the elements. That means you cannot use `forEach()`, `keys()`, `values()` or other methods to iterate over the elements.\\n`WeakSet` is ideally suited to scenarios where you have to track unique objects — when you want to hold temporary references to DOM elements or to objects in memory that can be cleaned up automatically when they are no longer needed, for example.",null],[658,"Recursion is the method in which a function calls itself in order to solve a task. It is often used to solve tasks that can be broken up into subtasks of a smaller size, such as computing a factorial, traversing trees or working with nested structures. For recursion to work you have to have an exit condition that will stop the endless loop.","Recursion in programming is the method in which a function calls itself in order to solve a problem, breaking it up into smaller subtasks. That lets you easily solve tasks that have a self-similar structure. The main aspects of recursion:\\n\\n- The structure of recursion: A recursive function must have:\\n\\n- An exit condition: This is the condition under which the function stops calling itself. Without an exit condition the recursion will continue endlessly, which will lead to a stack overflow error.\\n\\n- The recursive call: The function calls itself with changed arguments, in order to approach the exit condition gradually.\\n\\n- When to apply recursion:\\n\\n- The division of a task: When a task can be divided into smaller subtasks — when computing a factorial or finding Fibonacci numbers, for example.\\n\\n- Data structures: When working with hierarchical structures such as trees, where every node can contain subtrees, recursion can be convenient for traversing or modifying those structures.\\n\\n- An example of a recursive function: Let us look at computing the factorial of a number:\\n\\nIn this example the `factorial` function calls itself with a reduced value of `n`, until it reaches the base case `n === 0`.\\n\\n- The advantages and disadvantages:\\n\\n- The advantages: A recursive approach often leads to more concise and understandable code, especially for tasks connected with hierarchical structures.\\n\\n- The disadvantages: Recursion can be less effective compared with iterative solutions because of the overheads of function calls and the risk of a stack overflow.\\n\\nRecursion is a powerful tool in a developer\'s arsenal, which lets you solve complex tasks elegantly by means of simple, understandable logic.","function factorial(n) {\\n  if (n === 0) { // The exit condition  \\n    return 1;   \\n  } \\n  return n * factorial(n - 1); // The recursive call\\n} \\nconsole.log(factorial(5)); // 120"],[659,"A variable\'s scope determines where in the code that variable is available. In JavaScript there is global scope, block scope and function scope. Variables declared with `var` have function scope, while `let` and `const` have block scope, which means they are available only inside the block in which they were declared.","In JavaScript scope determines the context in which a variable is available for use. There are several kinds of scope:\\n\\n- Global scope: Variables declared outside functions or blocks of code become global and are available throughout the code. An example:\\n\\n- Function scope: Variables declared by means of `var` inside a function are available only in that function, but not beyond its bounds. An example:\\n\\n- Block scope: Variables declared by means of `let` and `const` have block scope, which means they are available only inside the block where they were declared — inside a loop or a conditional statement, for example:\\n\\n- Closures: Variables in closures are preserved even if their parent function has finished, which lets functions \\"remember\\" their surroundings.\\n\\nAn understanding of scope matters for preventing errors in code and for managing variables effectively.","let globalVar = \'I am global\'; \\nfunction printGlobal() {    \\n  console.log(globalVar); // The global variable is available \\n} \\nprintGlobal(); // \'I am global\'\\n\\nfunction funcScope() {\\n  var localVar = \'I am local\';    \\n  console.log(localVar); // Available inside the function \\n} \\nfuncScope(); \\nconsole.log(localVar); // Error: localVar is not defined\\n\\nif (true) {\\n  let blockVar = \'I am in block\';    \\n  console.log(blockVar); // Available inside the block \\n} \\nconsole.log(blockVar); // Error: blockVar is not defined"],[660,"An IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) is a function that is carried out immediately after it has been declared. That is achieved by wrapping the function in brackets and then calling it. An IIFE is often used to create an isolated scope, in order to avoid conflicts of variables in the global namespace.","An IIFE is a function that is called immediately after it is defined. Syntactically it looks like this:\\n\\nThe main idea is that you define a function and call it right there. That lets you create an isolated scope, so that the variables declared inside the IIFE will not be visible beyond its bounds, which helps avoid polluting the global scope. An example of the use of an IIFE:\\n\\nOne of the frequent applications of an IIFE in the past was creating closures to protect variables and prevent their conflicts with other parts of the code, especially before the appearance of block scope with `let` and `const`. An IIFE was used in old code to create isolated modules in JavaScript, for example.\\nToday IIFEs are met less often thanks to more modern tools for managing scope, such as `let`, `const` and ES6 modules, but they can still be useful for creating a temporary scope in special cases.","(function() {\\n  // the code inside the IIFE \\n})();\\n\\n(function() {\\n  let message = \'Hello, IIFE!\';\\n  console.log(message); // \'Hello, IIFE!\' \\n})(); \\nconsole.log(message); // Error: message is not defined"],[661,"In JavaScript functions are first-class objects. That means functions can be passed as arguments, returned from other functions and assigned to variables. The data type of a function is an object, and functions can have properties and methods, such as `.call()`, `.apply()` and `.bind()`.","In JavaScript functions are objects — more precisely, they are first-class objects. That means functions can be passed as arguments to other functions, returned as the result of functions, assigned to variables and held in data structures such as arrays or objects. Here is an example:\\n\\nFrom a technical point of view, the type of a function is an object. Functions have built-in methods and properties, such as:\\n\\n- `call()`: Lets you call a function with the context (`this`) explicitly stated.\\n\\n- `apply()`: The same as `call()`, but it takes the arguments in the form of an array.\\n\\n- `bind()`: Creates a new function with a bound context.\\n\\nAn example using `call()`:\\n\\nBesides that, since functions are objects, they can have their own properties:\\n\\nAn understanding that functions are objects extends their application, letting you use them more flexibly and dynamically.","function sayHello() {\\n    console.log(\'Hello!\'); \\n} \\nlet func = sayHello; // We assign the function to a variable \\nfunc(); // The call of the function through the variable\\n\\nfunction greet() {\\n  console.log(this.name); \\n} \\nconst user = { name: \'John\' }; \\ngreet.call(user); // \'John\', the `this` context points to the `user` object\\n\\nfunction counter() {\\n  counter.count++; \\n} \\ncounter.count = 0; \\ncounter(); \\nconsole.log(counter.count); // 1"],[662,"Arrow functions can use the `call`, `apply` and `bind` methods, but these methods do not change the `this` context inside an arrow function. Unlike ordinary functions, arrow functions inherit the value of `this` from their outer scope (the lexical context), and the `call`, `apply` and `bind` methods cannot change it.","Arrow functions in JavaScript have one key peculiarity — the lexical binding of the `this` context. That means that `this` in an arrow function does not depend on how and where it is called but always corresponds to the `this` in the scope in which the function was created. In connection with that, the `call`, `apply` and `bind` methods, which are usually used to change the context in ordinary functions, do not work for arrow functions in the usual sense.\\nAn example of an ordinary function:\\n\\nAn example of an arrow function:\\n\\nAlthough the `call`, `apply` and `bind` methods can be called on an arrow function, they have no effect whatsoever on the context. That makes arrow functions convenient for handling `this` — in the methods of a class or in event handlers, for example, where you do not have to take care of binding the context by hand.","function regularFunction() {\\n  console.log(this.name); \\n} \\nconst obj = { name: \'Alice\' }; \\nregularFunction.call(obj); // \'Alice\', the `this` context has been changed to obj\\n\\nconst arrowFunction = () => {\\n  console.log(this.name); \\n} \\nconst obj = { name: \'Alice\' }; \\narrowFunction.call(obj); // `this` does not change, the context stays the same as it was at the declaration"],[663,"If you change the parameters inside a function, their values will be changed only inside that function. At the same time, if a parameter is a primitive (a number, a string, a boolean value), the change will affect only the local copy, whereas if the parameter is an object, the changes will be reflected in the original object, because objects are passed by reference.","In JavaScript the parameters of a function can behave differently depending on their type — primitive or reference.\\n\\n- Primitive types (numbers, strings, boolean values): They are passed by value — that is, a copy of the value is created inside the function, and any changes to the parameter affect only that copy. An example:\\n\\n- Reference types (objects, arrays): They are passed by reference, which means that when the parameter is changed inside the function, the object itself that the reference points to is changed. An example:\\n\\nFrom that follows an important conclusion: primitive values are isolated inside the function, and changing them does not affect the original data, whereas objects are changed directly, which can lead to unforeseen consequences if this behaviour is not taken into account.","function modifyPrimitive(num) {\\n  num = num * 2;    \\n  console.log(num); // The locally changed value \\n} \\nlet x = 5; \\nmodifyPrimitive(x); // 10 \\nconsole.log(x); // 5, the original value has not changed\\n\\nfunction modifyObject(obj) {\\n  obj.name = \'Changed\'; \\n} \\nlet person = { name: \'Alice\' }; \\nmodifyObject(person); \\nconsole.log(person.name); // \'Changed\', the object has been changed"],[664,"Functions in JavaScript automatically receive the `name` and `length` properties. `name` holds the name of the function, while `length` states the number of parameters expected. These properties are set automatically and cannot be changed directly. The name of anonymous functions can be assigned through variables or when using arrow functions, however.","In JavaScript functions are objects, and they have several built-in properties, including `name` and `length`.\\nThe `name` property: This property returns the name of the function. It is assigned to the function automatically when it is defined. If the function is anonymous but has been assigned to a variable, then `name` will be the name of that variable.\\nFor example:\\n\\nFor arrow functions the name will be determined as the name of the variable they have been assigned to:\\n\\nThe `name` property cannot be changed directly, since it is read-only.\\nThe `length` property: This property shows the number of arguments the function expects to receive. For example:\\n\\nIf a function has arguments with default parameters or uses the `rest` operator, they are not taken into account in `length`:\\n\\nAlthough a function\'s `name` and `length` properties are established automatically, they are useful for analysing code or for debugging, since they let you obtain metadata about the function.","function myFunction() {} \\nconsole.log(myFunction.name); // \'myFunction\' \\n\\nconst anonymousFunction = function() {}; \\nconsole.log(anonymousFunction.name); // \'anonymousFunction\'\\n\\nconst arrowFunc = () => {}; \\nconsole.log(arrowFunc.name); // \'arrowFunc\'\\n\\nfunction example(a, b, c) {} \\nconsole.log(example.length); // 3\\n\\nfunction example(a, b = 2) {} \\nconsole.log(example.length); // 1"],[665,"`setTimeout` carries out the function given once after a given time, while `setInterval` does so periodically, at the interval given. Both methods work asynchronously, which means they do not block the main thread of the program\'s execution. To stop the execution of `setInterval` you have to call `clearInterval`, and to stop a postponed call of `setTimeout` — `clearTimeout`.","In JavaScript `setTimeout` and `setInterval` are methods used for the asynchronous execution of code after a particular time.\\n\\n- `setTimeout` carries out a function once after a given number of milliseconds. For example:\\n\\nIf you have to cancel a scheduled call, you can use `clearTimeout`:\\n\\n- `setInterval` calls a function at a given time interval. For example:\\n\\nTo stop the repeating call you have to call `clearInterval`:\\n\\nThese methods matter for carrying out tasks with a delay or with repetition — for creating timers, animations or regular updates of data, for example.","setTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"Hello after 2 seconds!\\"); \\n}, 2000); // Will be carried out after 2 seconds\\n\\nlet timeoutID = setTimeout(() => console.log(\\"Hello\\"), 2000); \\nclearTimeout(timeoutID); // Cancels the execution\\n\\nlet intervalID = setInterval(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"A repeating message every 3 seconds\\"); \\n}, 3000);\\n\\nclearInterval(intervalID);"],[666,"In JavaScript OOP is based on principles such as encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction. Encapsulation consists in combining data and methods in a single object. Inheritance lets one class inherit the properties and methods of another. Polymorphism lets you use one and the same method in different contexts, while abstraction hides the complex details of the implementation from the user.","Object-oriented programming (OOP) in JavaScript is implemented through classes and objects. The main principles of OOP include:\\n\\n- Encapsulation — this is the combining of data and methods in a single object. It helps hide an object\'s internal logic and provides interfaces for interacting with it:\\n\\n- Inheritance — this is the ability of one class to inherit the properties and methods of another. For example:\\n\\n- Polymorphism — this is the ability of objects of different classes to react to one and the same method differently. In the example above the `speak` method works differently for the `Dog` class and the base `Animal` class.\\n\\n- Abstraction — this is the process of hiding complex details and providing the user with only the interfaces necessary for working with the objects.\\n\\nJavaScript provides powerful means for working with OOP, which lets you build scalable and easily maintainable applications.","class Person {\\n  constructor(name, age) {\\n    this.name = name;        \\n    this.age = age;    \\n  }    \\n  greet() {\\n    return `Hello, my name is ${this.name}`;    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst john = new Person(\'John\', 30); \\nconsole.log(john.greet()); // \'Hello, my name is John\'\\n\\nclass Animal {\\n  speak() {\\n    return \\"Animal sound\\";    \\n  } \\n} \\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  speak() {        \\n    return \\"Bark\\";    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst dog = new Dog(); \\nconsole.log(dog.speak()); // \'Bark\'"],[667,"The main principles of functional programming include pure functions, the immutability of data, functions as first-class objects and the absence of side effects. Pure functions always return the same result for the same input data. Immutability means that data is not changed but new copies are created. First-class functions can be passed as arguments and returned as the result of other functions.","Functional programming (FP) is a paradigm based on the use of functions as the main building blocks of programs. Here are the key principles of FP:\\n\\n- Pure functions: A pure function is a function that always returns one and the same result for the same input data and has no side effects. For example:\\n\\n- Immutability: In FP data is not changed directly. Instead, new copies of objects are created with updated values. That reduces the number of errors connected with changing data:\\n\\n- Functions as first-class objects: In JavaScript functions can be passed as arguments to other functions, returned as a result and assigned to variables. That lets you create powerful constructions of the higher-order function kind:\\n\\n- The absence of side effects: Side effects are any changes a function produces outside itself — changing global variables or output to the console, for example. FP aims to minimise such effects, so that the code is predictable.\\n\\nFP helps you write clean, easily testable and scalable code.","function add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b; \\n}\\n\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3]; \\nconst newArr = [...arr, 4]; // A new array is created\\n\\nfunction applyFunction(func, value) {\\n  return func(value); \\n} \\nconst square = x => x * x; \\nconsole.log(applyFunction(square, 5)); // 25"],[668,"The `call` and `apply` methods let you call a function with a given value of `this`, but `call` passes the arguments separated by commas while `apply` passes them in the form of an array. The `bind` method returns a new function with a fixed value of `this`, which can be called later. These methods are useful for controlling the context in which a function is executed, especially when working with the methods of objects.","In JavaScript the `call`, `apply` and `bind` methods are used to state the value of `this` explicitly when calling a function.\\n\\n- `call`: This method calls a function with the value of `this` given and with the arguments passed separated by commas:\\n\\n- `apply`: Similar to `call`, but the arguments are passed in the form of an array:\\n\\n- `bind`: Returns a new function with the value of `this` given, which can be called later:\\n\\nThese methods matter for working with functions in the context of other objects and let you manage how and where a function will be executed more flexibly.","function greet(greeting) {\\n  console.log(greeting + \', \' + this.name); \\n} \\nconst user = { name: \'Alice\' }; \\ngreet.call(user, \'Hello\'); // Will print: \'Hello, Alice\'\\n\\ngreet.apply(user, [\'Hi\']); // Will print: \'Hi, Alice\'\\n\\nconst boundGreet = greet.bind(user); \\nboundGreet(\'Hey\'); // Will print: \'Hey, Alice\'"],[669,"Arrow functions do not create their own `this` context and use the value of `this` from the outer function. They also have no `arguments` object, which makes them less flexible in some situations. The context of an arrow function cannot be changed, since it is rigidly bound to the outer scope.","Arrow functions in JavaScript have several peculiarities compared with ordinary functions:\\n`this` in arrow functions: Arrow functions have no value of `this` of their own. Instead they use the value of `this` from the surrounding context:\\n\\nThat makes arrow functions useful in cases where it matters to preserve the context from an outer function — when using methods inside event handlers, for example.\\n`arguments`: Arrow functions have no `arguments` object, which contains all of the arguments passed into the function. If access to the arguments is needed, you can use the `...args` parameter (the rest operator):\\n\\nThe impossibility of changing the context: Unlike ordinary functions, arrow functions ignore the `call`, `apply` and `bind` methods — changing their context is impossible. That makes them more predictable as regards the context, but less flexible.\\nArrow functions are useful for writing short, simple functions that do not depend on changing the context. If you have to control the context or work with `arguments`, however, it is better to use ordinary functions.","const obj = {\\n  name: \'Alice\',    \\n  greet: () => {     \\n    console.log(this.name); // In an arrow function `this` is taken from the outer context — the global object    \\n  } \\n}; \\nobj.greet(); // Will print undefined, since `this` points to the global object\\n\\nconst sum = (...args) => args.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0); \\nconsole.log(sum(1, 2, 3)); // 6"],[670,"Prototypal inheritance is the mechanism in JavaScript by which objects can inherit properties and methods from other objects through the chain of prototypes. Every object in JavaScript has a hidden reference to its prototype, which lets you gain access to properties and methods that are not defined directly on the object. The main mechanism of inheritance in JavaScript is based on the link through prototypes rather than on classes, as in other programming languages.","Prototypal inheritance is a key concept in JavaScript, letting objects inherit properties and methods from other objects through what is called the prototype chain. When you try to gain access to a property or a method of an object, JavaScript first checks whether it exists on the object itself. If it does not, the JavaScript engine moves to that object\'s prototype and checks there. This process continues along the chain until the property needed is found or the chain of prototypes ends.\\nEvery object in JavaScript has a hidden `[[Prototype]]` property, accessible through `__proto__`, which refers to its prototype — the object from which it inherits. If an object has to have access to particular methods or properties, they can be added to its prototype, and every object inheriting from it will gain access to them.\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhen we call `dog.speak()`, JavaScript first checks whether the `dog` object has a `speak` method. Not finding it, the engine turns to the prototype of the `dog` object, which refers to the `animal` object, where this method is indeed found.\\nThe chain of prototypes lets you save memory, since methods and properties are not duplicated on every object but inherited through the prototypes.","const animal = {\\n  speak() {    \\n    console.log(\'Animal speaks\');    \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst dog = {\\n  bark() {     \\n    console.log(\'Dog barks\');    \\n  } \\n}; // We establish animal as the prototype for dog \\ndog.__proto__ = animal; // Now dog can use the speak method \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\'"],[671,"A prototype in JavaScript is an object from which other objects can inherit properties and methods. You can assign a prototype by means of the `Object.create()` method or through the `__proto__` property. That lets you organise inheritance and share common methods and properties between several objects.","A prototype is an object used as the source for the inheritance of properties and methods by other objects. The main aim of a prototype is to provide a common set of functions and properties that can be reused in several objects without duplicating their code.\\nThere are several ways of assigning a prototype to an object:\\n\\n- By means of the `Object.create()` method: This method lets you create a new object and state its prototype explicitly:\\n\\n- Through the `__proto__` property: This is an obsolete but still used way of explicitly assigning a prototype to an object:\\n\\n- Through a constructor function and the `prototype` property: If a constructor function is used, its prototype is automatically assigned to the objects created:\\n\\nAssigning a prototype lets you create objects that share common methods, avoiding the duplication of code and making maintenance simpler.","const animal = {\\n  speak() {     \\n    console.log(\'Animal speaks\');    \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst dog = Object.create(animal); \\ndog.bark = function() {\\n  console.log(\'Dog barks\'); \\n}; \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\' \\ndog.bark();  // \'Dog barks\'\\n\\nconst animal = {\\n  speak() {     \\n    console.log(\'Animal speaks\');    \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst dog = {\\n  bark() {    \\n    console.log(\'Dog barks\');    \\n  } \\n}; \\ndog.__proto__ = animal;  // We establish animal as the prototype for dog \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\'\\n\\nfunction Animal() {} \\nAnimal.prototype.speak = function() {\\n  console.log(\'Animal speaks\'); \\n}; \\nconst dog = new Animal(); \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\'"],[672,"`__proto__` is a hidden property of every object, which refers to its prototype, letting it inherit properties and methods. `prototype` is a property of constructor functions, which determines the prototype for every object created by means of them. When an object is created by means of a constructor, it receives a reference to the prototype through the `__proto__` property, while the prototype of those objects is set through the constructor\'s `prototype` property.","In JavaScript there are two related but different concepts — `__proto__` and `prototype`.\\n\\n- `__proto__`: This is a hidden internal property present on every object. It refers to the object\'s prototype, from which it inherits properties and methods. This property is used to implement the mechanism of prototypal inheritance. Through it JavaScript determines which object to turn to if the property required is not found on the object itself.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n`__proto__` can be used to change the chain of prototypes dynamically, although that is not considered good practice because of potential problems with performance.\\n\\n- `prototype`: This is a property of constructor functions, which is used to determine the prototype of every object created by means of that constructor. If you create an object through a constructor function, its hidden `__proto__` property will refer to the object defined through the constructor function\'s `prototype`.\\n\\nAn example using a constructor function:\\n\\nHere `dog.__proto__` refers to `Animal.prototype`, which lets the `dog` object inherit the `speak` method.\\n`__proto__` thus controls the reference to the prototype for any object, whereas `prototype` controls what will be established as the prototype for the objects created by means of a constructor.","const obj = {}; \\nconsole.log(obj.__proto__);  // Will print Object.prototype\\n\\nfunction Animal() {} \\nAnimal.prototype.speak = function() {\\n  console.log(\'Animal speaks\'); \\n}; \\nconst dog = new Animal(); \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\'"],[673,"When a method is called on an object that inherits that method through a prototype, `this` points to the object on which the method was called rather than to the prototype itself. That is, regardless of where the method is defined — on the object or on its prototype — `this` always refers to the object that initiated the call.","In JavaScript a key peculiarity of prototypal inheritance is that when a method inherited through a prototype is called, the context (`this`) always refers to the object that directly calls that method rather than to the prototype itself. That matters, since `this` points to the current object in the context of the function\'s call.\\nFor example:\\n\\nHere the `speak` method was inherited from the `animal` object, but when it is called through the `dog` object, `this` refers to `dog`. `this` thus works with the object that called the method rather than with the prototype object.\\nThis behaviour lets you use common methods for different objects while preserving the correct binding of the context.","const animal = {\\n  speak() {\\n    console.log(this.name + \' speaks\');  \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst dog = {  name: \'Rex\' }; \\ndog.__proto__ = animal; \\ndog.speak(); // \'Rex speaks\'"],[674,"In JavaScript primitives such as strings, numbers and boolean values are temporarily wrapped in the corresponding object types (`String`, `Number`, `Boolean`) when their methods are accessed. These objects give access to the methods and properties, after which the primitive turns back into its original value.","Although primitives in JavaScript, such as numbers, strings and boolean values, are not objects, they behave as though they had methods and properties. That happens thanks to a mechanism called \\"autoboxing\\". When you call a method on a primitive, JavaScript automatically creates a temporary object of the corresponding type.\\nFor example:\\n\\nIn this example `str` is a primitive string, but when the `toUpperCase()` method is called, JavaScript creates a temporary object of the `String` type behind the scenes, on which that method is carried out. Once the call has finished the object is destroyed, and the variable `str` becomes an ordinary string again.\\nThis mechanism works for every primitive:\\n\\n- Strings are wrapped in `String` objects.\\n\\n- Numbers — in `Number` objects.\\n\\n- Boolean values — in `Boolean` objects.\\n\\nThe primitive objects give access to methods such as `toUpperCase()` for strings, `toFixed()` for numbers and other methods, which would otherwise not be available for primitives.","let str = \'hello\'; \\nconsole.log(str.toUpperCase()); // \'HELLO\'"],[675,"Inheritance in JavaScript can be implemented through prototypes, by linking objects with one another. That can be done by hand by means of the `__proto__` property or using the `Object.create()` method. It lets a new object gain access to the properties and methods of another object through the chain of prototypes.","In JavaScript inheritance can be implemented by means of prototypes. Every object can have a reference to another object through the hidden `[[Prototype]]` property, accessible through `__proto__`. When you try to gain access to a property that does not exist on the object, JavaScript turns to its prototype and then continues the search along the chain of prototypes.\\nThere are several ways of creating inheritance:\\n\\n- The use of `__proto__`: This is the most straightforward way of linking objects.\\n\\n- The use of `Object.create()`: This method creates a new object and lets you state its prototype explicitly.\\n\\n- Inheritance through constructor functions: In JavaScript you can also use constructor functions to create objects that will inherit properties and methods through the `prototype` property:\\n\\nInheritance through prototypes is a flexible mechanism, which lets you create objects that share common methods and properties, while preserving low memory consumption and the ability to change objects\' behaviour dynamically.","const animal = {\\n  speak() {\\n    console.log(\'Animal speaks\');  \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst dog = {\\n  bark() {\\n    console.log(\'Dog barks\');  \\n  } \\n};\\ndog.__proto__ = animal; \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\' \\ndog.bark();  // \'Dog barks\'\\n\\nconst animal = {\\n  speak() { \\n    console.log(\'Animal speaks\');  \\n  } \\n}; \\nconst dog = Object.create(animal); \\ndog.bark = function() {\\n  console.log(\'Dog barks\'); \\n}; \\ndog.speak(); // \'Animal speaks\' \\ndog.bark();  // \'Dog barks\'\\n\\nfunction Animal() {\\n  Animal.prototype.speak = function() {\\n    console.log(\'Animal speaks\');\\n  };\\n}\\nfunction Dog() {}\\nDog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype);\\nDog.prototype.constructor = Dog;\\nconst dog = new Dog();\\ndog.speak(); // Animal speaks"],[676,"To check whether an object is an instance of a particular class or constructor function, the `instanceof` operator is used. It checks whether there is a reference to the constructor\'s prototype in the object\'s chain of prototypes.","The `instanceof` operator in JavaScript lets you check whether an object was created by means of a particular constructor function or inherited from it through prototypes. It works along the object\'s chain of prototypes, checking whether it has a reference to the constructor function\'s prototype among its prototypes.\\nAn example:\\n\\nHere the `myDog` object was created through the `Dog` constructor, but thanks to the inheritance from `Animal` it is also an instance of `Animal`. The `instanceof` operator goes along the chain of prototypes and checks whether they refer to the corresponding prototype.\\nThis operator is useful for determining the types of objects and working with them depending on their origin.","function Animal() {} \\nfunction Dog() {} \\n\\nDog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype); \\n\\nconst myDog = new Dog(); \\nconsole.log(myDog instanceof Dog); // true \\n\\nconsole.log(myDog instanceof Animal); // true \\nconsole.log(myDog instanceof Object); // true"],[677,"To check whether an object has a particular property, you can use the `in` operator, which checks both the object itself and its prototypes. If you have to check only for the presence of a property directly in the object (without taking the prototypes into account), the `hasOwnProperty()` method is used.","In JavaScript there are several ways of checking for the presence of a property in an object. The `in` operator checks both the object\'s properties and the properties that may be inherited from its prototypes.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIf you have to check whether a property belongs to the object itself, without taking its prototypes into account, you can use the `hasOwnProperty()` method:\\n\\nThis method checks only the properties belonging directly to the object. It is useful if you want to separate an object\'s own properties from those inherited through prototypes.","const animal = { canMove: true }; \\nconst dog = Object.create(animal); \\n\\ndog.barks = true; \\n\\nconsole.log(\'barks\' in dog); // true \\nconsole.log(\'canMove\' in dog); // true (inherited from animal)\\n\\nconsole.log(dog.hasOwnProperty(\'barks\')); // true \\nconsole.log(dog.hasOwnProperty(\'canMove\')); // false"],[678,"A callback is a function that is passed into another function as an argument and called later, when some event happens or an operation finishes. The \\"Callback Hell\\" problem arises when functions start to nest inside one another too deeply, which makes the code hard to read and difficult to maintain.","In JavaScript callbacks are widely used for working with asynchronous code. When you make an HTTP request or work with timers, for example, you pass a function that will be called later, when the request finishes or the time expires.\\nAn example of the use of a callback:\\n\\nHere the `fetchData` function uses the `setTimeout` timer, and the callback is called after the time has expired.\\nThe use of a large number of nested callbacks can lead to the problem known as \\"Callback Hell\\", however. This is the situation where functions nest inside one another too deeply and the code becomes difficult to understand:\\n\\nThat makes the code hard to maintain, and debugging can become difficult. To solve this problem other approaches were introduced, such as `Promises` and `async/await`, which let you write asynchronous code in a more linear and understandable form.","function fetchData(callback) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    const data = \'Server response\';    \\n    callback(data);  \\n  }, 1000); \\n} \\nfetchData((response) => {\\n  console.log(response); // \'Server response\' \\n});\\n\\ndoSomething((result1) => {\\n  doSomethingElse(result1, (result2) => {\\n    doAnotherThing(result2, (result3) => { \\n      doFinalThing(result3, (finalResult) => {    \\n        console.log(finalResult);      \\n      });    \\n    });  \\n  }); \\n});"],[679,"A Promise is an object representing the result of an asynchronous operation, which can finish successfully (with a result) or with an error. Promises help avoid \\"callback hell\\" and let you write asynchronous code in a more manageable and linear form.","A promise is a special object in JavaScript, which is used to handle asynchronous operations such as requests to a server. A promise can be in one of three states: pending, fulfilled or rejected. When the operation has finished, the promise either returns the result or reports an error.\\nAn example of creating and using a promise:\\n\\nPromises are used to improve the readability and the convenience of working with asynchronous operations, which lets you avoid complex nestings of callbacks and makes the code more predictable.","const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => { \\n    const success = true; // the condition for fulfilment    \\n    if (success) { \\n      resolve(\'Operation completed successfully\');    \\n    } else { \\n      reject(\'Error occurred\');    \\n    }  \\n  }, 1000); \\n}); \\nmyPromise.then((result) => {\\n  console.log(result); // \'Operation completed successfully\' \\n})\\n.catch((error) => {  console.error(error); });"],[680,"The main property of a promise is its state (pending, fulfilled, rejected). The methods of a promise include `then()`, which is used to handle a successful result, `catch()` for handling errors and `finally()` for carrying out code after the promise has finished, regardless of its outcome.","Promises have three states:\\n\\n- Pending — the operation is in the process of being carried out.\\n\\n- Fulfilled — the operation has finished successfully.\\n\\n- Rejected — the operation finished with an error.\\n\\nThe main methods of promises:\\n\\n- `then(onFulfilled, onRejected)` — used to handle the successful fulfilment of the promise or an error. The first argument is the function that fires on successful completion, the second a function for handling an error.\\n\\n- `catch(onRejected)` — lets you handle an error (it is a shortened version of `then(null, onRejected)`).\\n\\n- `finally(onFinally)` — is carried out after the promise has either been fulfilled or rejected, regardless of the result. That is convenient for cleaning up resources, finishing an animation or other tasks that have to be done in any case.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThese methods help you handle asynchronous operations in a clean, linear form, making the code simpler and improving its maintenance.","myPromise  \\n  .then(result => console.log(result))  \\n  .catch(error => console.error(error))  \\n  .finally(() => console.log(\'Operation complete.\'));"],[681,"A chain of promises lets you carry out several asynchronous operations one after another. Every call of `then()` can return a new promise, which will be passed into the next `then()`, which makes handling complex asynchronous tasks simpler.","Promise chaining is the mechanism that lets you call several asynchronous operations one after another. After one promise has been fulfilled successfully you can return a new promise, which will be handled in the next `then()`. That is especially useful when one operation depends on the results of the previous one.\\nAn example of a chain of promises:\\n\\nA chain of promises makes asynchronous processes simpler, letting you manage sequential operations without deep nesting of functions. It makes the code cleaner, more linear and easily manageable.","function fetchData() {\\n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\\n    setTimeout(() => resolve(\'Data from server\'), 1000);  \\n  }); \\n} \\nfunction processData(data) {\\n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\\n    setTimeout(() => resolve(`${data} processed`), 1000);  \\n  }); \\n} \\nfetchData()  \\n.then((data) => {\\n  console.log(data); // \'Data from server\'    \\n  return processData(data);  \\n})  \\n.then((processedData) => {\\n  console.log(processedData); // \'Data from server processed\'  \\n})  \\n.catch((error) => {\\n  console.error(error);  \\n});"],[682,"Promisification is the process of converting functions that use callbacks into functions that return promises. That makes working with asynchronous code more convenient and makes it simpler to read and manage. Promisification lets you use promises and their chains instead of deep nesting of callbacks.","Promisification is the approach in which asynchronous functions using a callback are converted into functions that return a promise. Such a method makes working with asynchronous code simpler, making it more readable and structured. If previously you had to use nested callbacks to handle the result of asynchronous operations, for example, promisification lets you \\"unfold\\" them into chains of `then()`, improving the maintenance and the extensibility of the code.\\nAn example of promisification:\\n\\nPromisification lets you handle errors by means of `catch()`, avoiding complex constructions with callbacks and improving the readability and the predictability of asynchronous code.","// An ordinary function with a callback \\nfunction loadData(callback) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    callback(null, \'Data loaded\');  \\n  }, 1000); \\n} \\n\\n// The promisified version \\nfunction loadDataPromise() {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      resolve(\'Data loaded\');    \\n    }, 1000);  \\n  }); \\n} \\nloadDataPromise().then((data) => console.log(data)); // \'Data loaded\'"],[683,"The main methods of a promise include: `then()` — for handling successful fulfilment, `catch()` — for handling errors, `finally()` — for carrying out code once the promise has finished, regardless of the result. There are also the methods `Promise.all()`, `Promise.race()`, `Promise.allSettled()` and `Promise.any()`, which let you work with several promises at once.","The `Promise` object in JavaScript provides several methods for managing asynchronous operations:\\n\\n- `then(onFulfilled, onRejected)` — used to handle the successful fulfilment of a promise or errors.\\n\\n- `catch(onRejected)` — catches errors that happened in the promise or in one of the subsequent `then()`s.\\n\\n- `finally(onFinally)` — is carried out in any case, when the promise has finished (successfully or with an error).\\n\\nMethods for working with a multitude of promises:\\n\\n- `Promise.all(promises)` — takes an array of promises and returns a new promise, which will be fulfilled only when all of the promises passed have finished. If at least one promise is rejected, `Promise.all` will reject the result.\\n\\n- `Promise.race(promises)` — returns a promise that will be fulfilled or rejected first among the promises passed.\\n\\n- `Promise.allSettled(promises)` — returns a new promise, which will be fulfilled when all of the promises passed have finished (successfully or with an error), and returns an array of objects with the results.\\n\\n- `Promise.any(promises)` — returns the first successful promise among those passed, ignoring the rejected promises.\\n\\nAn example of the use of the `Promise.all()` method:\\n\\nThese methods provide flexible tools for working with several asynchronous operations, letting you control their execution and handling.","const promise1 = Promise.resolve(10); \\nconst promise2 = Promise.resolve(20); \\nconst promise3 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000, 30)); \\nPromise.all([promise1, promise2, promise3]).then((values) => {\\n  console.log(values); // [10, 20, 30] \\n});"],[684,"`async/await` is a syntax that makes working with promises simpler, making asynchronous code more like synchronous code. A function with the `async` keyword always returns a promise, while `await` suspends the execution of the function until the result of the promise is received. That makes the code more readable and makes handling errors simpler.","`async/await` is an improved way of working with asynchronous operations in JavaScript, based on promises. A function declared with the `async` keyword always returns a promise. Inside such a function you can use the `await` operator, which suspends the execution until the promise has been fulfilled (or rejected). That lets you write asynchronous code in a synchronous style, which improves its readability and reduces the likelihood of errors.\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example the code is carried out sequentially, but thanks to `await` it \\"waits\\" for the asynchronous operations to finish (the response from the server, for example). If an error arises, it is handled by means of a `try/catch` block. `async/await` makes the code more understandable and makes handling errors simpler compared with chains of `then/catch`.","async function fetchData() {\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1\');    \\n    const data = await response.json();    \\n    console.log(data);  \\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(\'Error:\', error); \\n  } \\n} \\nfetchData();"],[685,"In JavaScript errors are handled by means of the `try...catch` block. In the `try` block the code that may raise an error is placed, and in the `catch` block the instructions for handling that error. You can also use `finally` in order to carry out code regardless of whether an error arose or not. Error handling can be extended by means of custom errors.","In JavaScript, to handle errors the `try...catch` construction is used, which lets you \\"catch\\" errors arising during the execution of a program and manage their consequences. If an error arises inside the `try` block, control is passed to the `catch` block, which takes the error object and can handle it or display a message for the user.\\nAn example:\\n\\nBesides that, you can create your own errors by means of the `throw` operator, for example:\\n\\nThe error object contains several properties:\\n\\n- name — the type of the error (`ReferenceError`, for example).\\n\\n- message — a description of the error.\\n\\n- stack — the trace of the call stack (useful for debugging).\\n\\nSuch an approach lets you avoid unexpected crashes of a program and gives you the ability to control the behaviour in the event of errors.","try {\\n  let result = someUndefinedFunction(); // An error, since the function is not defined \\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.error(\\"An error occurred:\\", error.message); \\n} finally {\\n  console.log(\\"This code will be carried out in any case.\\"); \\n}\\n\\nfunction checkAge(age) {\\n  if (age < 18) {\\n    throw new Error(\\"The age must be greater than 18.\\");  \\n  } \\n}"],[686,"The error object in JavaScript contains information that helps diagnose problems. The key properties of the error object are `name` (the type of the error), `message` (the text of the error) and `stack` (the trace of the calls leading to the error). This data is useful for revealing the cause of an error and eliminating it.","The error object in JavaScript is an important tool for debugging and diagnosis. When an error arises, JavaScript creates an error object, which contains several properties:\\n\\n- `name` — points to the type of the error (`ReferenceError`, `TypeError`, for example).\\n\\n- `message` — contains a description of the error, which explains what went wrong.\\n\\n- `stack` — the stack trace, showing the sequence of function calls that led to the error. That is useful for determining the place in the code where the error arose.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHandling such objects lets developers find and correct errors in code effectively, and also improve its resilience to potential failures.","try {\\n  nonExistentFunction(); // The call of a non-existent function \\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.log(error.name); // ReferenceError  \\n  console.log(error.message); // nonExistentFunction is not defined  \\n  console.log(error.stack); // the stack trace \\n}"],[687,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a textual format for representing data in the form of objects and arrays. It is used to exchange data between a client and a server. To work with JSON in JavaScript the methods `JSON.stringify()` for converting an object into a string and `JSON.parse()` for converting a string into an object are used.","JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a data exchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript. It is lightweight, human-readable and widely used to transfer data between a server and a client in web applications.\\nData in the JSON format is held in the form of \\"key-value\\" pairs, which makes it similar to objects in JavaScript. JSON supports simple data types such as strings, numbers, arrays and objects.\\nAn example of JSON:\\n\\nTo work with JSON in JavaScript two methods are used:\\n\\n- `JSON.stringify(object)` — converts a JavaScript object into a JSON string. That is convenient when data has to be sent to a server.\\n\\n- `JSON.parse(jsonString)` — converts a JSON string back into a JavaScript object. That is useful when receiving data from a server.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nJSON is widely used in REST APIs and is the standard for transferring data in web applications, since it is simple to process and well supported.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"John\\",  \\n  \\"age\\": 30,  \\n  \\"isStudent\\": false,  \\n  \\"courses\\": [\\"Math\\", \\"Science\\"] \\n}\\n\\nconst user = { name: \\"Alice\\", age: 25 }; \\nconst jsonString = JSON.stringify(user); \\nconsole.log(jsonString); // \'{\\"name\\":\\"Alice\\",\\"age\\":25}\'\\n \\nconst parsedData = JSON.parse(jsonString); \\nconsole.log(parsedData.name); // \'Alice\'"],[688,"Classes in JavaScript represent syntactic sugar for prototypal inheritance. When a class is created the JavaScript interpreter creates an object and links it with the class\'s prototype, letting it inherit its methods and properties. Every instance of the class gains access to those methods through the chain of prototypes, which ensures an effective use of memory.","JavaScript classes are a world of internal mechanisms and prototypal inheritance. Let us work out what happens \\"under the hood\\" when you create a class.\\nPrototypes: the root of everything\\nBefore moving on to classes, let us recall prototypes. In JavaScript classes are in essence a beautiful wrapper over prototypal inheritance.\\n\\nA class under the microscope\\nWhen you write a modern class:\\n\\nInside, roughly the following happens:\\n\\n- The constructor: The constructor method turns into a constructor function.\\n\\n- The methods of the instance: They are added to the class\'s prototype.\\n\\n- Static methods: They are bound directly to the class.\\n\\nThe magic of creating an object: when you write new User(\'Ivan\'), the following happens:\\n\\n- A new object is created\\n\\n- The `this` context is bound to that object\\n\\n- The constructor is called\\n\\n- The object is returned (unless stated otherwise)\\n\\nInheritance: under the hood\\n\\nWhen inheriting, JavaScript:\\n\\n- Establishes the prototype chain\\n\\n- Copies the static methods\\n\\nIt preserves the link between the classes\\nSubtleties and pitfalls\\n\\n- Classes are functions:\\n\\n- Classes are not hoisted:\\n\\n- Methods are not enumerable by default:\\n\\nA bonus: how this works at the level of the engine\\nThe V8 engine (Chrome, Node.js) optimises classes:\\n\\n- It caches prototypes\\n\\n- It uses fast internal mechanisms for creating objects\\n\\n- It applies special optimisations for frequent operations\\n\\nConclusion\\nClasses in JavaScript are a powerful tool, which hides the complex mechanics of prototypal inheritance behind a simple, understandable syntax. An understanding of what happens \\"under the hood\\" will help you write more effective and more deliberate code.","// The old way, before classes\\nfunction User(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n}\\nUser.prototype.sayHi = function() {\\n  console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n};\\nconst user = new User(\'Ivan\');\\nuser.sayHi(); // Works just like a modern class\\n\\nclass User {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n  sayHi() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n  }\\n  static createAnonymous() {\\n    return new User(\'Anonymous\');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Employee extends User {\\n  constructor(name, position) {\\n    super(name);  // The call of the parent constructor\\n    this.position = position;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass MyClass {}\\nconsole.log(typeof MyClass); // \\"function\\"\\n\\nconst obj = new MyClass(); // ReferenceError\\nclass MyClass {}\\n\\nclass Example {\\n  method() {} // Will not be visible in for...in\\n}"],[689,"When a class is created in JavaScript the interpreter creates a constructor function and establishes its prototype. The class\'s methods are added to the prototype, which lets every instance of the class use one and the same method, saving memory. That makes classes effective for creating objects with common behaviours.","When you declare a class in JavaScript, the process includes several steps. First of all a constructor function is created, which initialises objects by means of the `constructor` method. All of the methods declared in the class are in fact added to its prototype. That means every instance of the class has access to those methods through the chain of prototypes, which saves memory.\\nHere is an example:\\n\\nIn this example the `Car` class has a `drive` method, which can be called on any instance of `Car`. That demonstrates how methods are linked with instances using prototypal inheritance, which lets you use memory effectively and organise the code.","class Car {    \\n  constructor(brand) {        \\n    this.brand = brand;    \\n  } \\n  \\n  drive() {        \\n    console.log(`The ${this.brand} is driving.`);    \\n  } \\n} \\n\\nconst myCar = new Car(\'Toyota\'); \\nmyCar.drive(); // The Toyota is driving."],[690,"Classes are templates for creating objects with common properties and methods in JavaScript. They let you organise code and implement inheritance. The keywords connected with classes include `class`, `constructor`, `extends` and `super`, which help you create and manage classes and their instances.","Classes in JavaScript are syntactic sugar, which makes creating objects and implementing object-oriented programming simpler. Classes let you create new types of object with common properties and methods, which makes the code more organised and convenient to maintain.\\nThe keywords connected with classes:\\n\\n- `class`: used to define a new class.\\n\\n- `constructor`: a special method that is called when a new instance of the class is created.\\n\\n- `extends`: lets one class inherit the properties and methods of another class.\\n\\n- `super`: used to call the constructor of the parent class and to access its methods.\\n\\nHere is a simple example:\\n\\nIn this example `Dog` inherits `Animal` and overrides the `speak` method. Using keywords such as `extends` and `super`, we can easily create hierarchies of classes and manage their behaviour, which makes the code more understandable and modular.","class Animal {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n    }  \\n      \\n  speak() {        \\n    console.log(`${this.name} makes a noise.`);    \\n  } \\n} \\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  constructor(name) {        \\n    super(name); // The call of the parent class\'s constructor    \\n  }    \\n  \\n  speak() {        \\n    console.log(`${this.name} barks.`);\\n  }\\n} \\n\\nconst dog = new Dog(\'Rex\'); \\ndog.speak(); // Rex barks."],[691,"Protected properties in JavaScript are properties whose access is restricted for outside code but which are available to the object\'s methods. That lets you hide an object\'s internal data and prevent its accidental change. Protected properties can be implemented by means of symbols, a WeakMap or through closures.","Protected properties help implement encapsulation by restricting access to an object\'s data. That lets you build safer programs that are more resilient to errors. In JavaScript there is no built-in support for protected properties, but you can use various approaches to implement them.\\nThe use of symbols: Symbols are unique and cannot be changed accidentally, which lets you create protected properties.\\n\\nThe use of a WeakMap: A WeakMap lets you hold data available only inside the methods of a class.\\n\\nClosures: You can create protected properties by means of closures, which create a scope inaccessible from outside.\\n\\nProtected properties thus help control access to data and raise the security of the code.","const protectedProp = Symbol(\'protectedProp\'); \\nclass Example {\\n  constructor(value) {    \\n    this[protectedProp] = value;    \\n  }    \\n  getProtectedProp() {    \\n    return this[protectedProp];    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst obj = new Example(\'secret\'); \\nconsole.log(obj.getProtectedProp()); // secret \\nconsole.log(obj[protectedProp]); // undefined\\n\\nconst privateData = new WeakMap(); \\nclass Example {\\n  constructor(value) {     \\n    privateData.set(this, value);    \\n  }    \\n  getPrivateData() {     \\n    return privateData.get(this);    \\n  } \\n} \\nconst obj = new Example(\'hidden\'); \\nconsole.log(obj.getPrivateData()); // hidden\\n\\nfunction createExample(value) {\\n  let protectedProp = value;    \\n  return {    \\n    getProtectedProp() {     \\n        return protectedProp;        \\n      }    \\n  }; \\n} \\nconst obj = createExample(\'hidden\'); \\nconsole.log(obj.getProtectedProp()); // hidden \\nconsole.log(obj.protectedProp); // undefined"],[692,"In a hash table using the chaining method to resolve collisions, every bucket represents a data structure, usually a linked list. When the hash function returns an index for a key, we turn to the corresponding bucket. The search inside the bucket is carried out by a linear pass over the elements of that list. We compare the key sought with the key of every element in the list, until we find a match or reach the end of the list. The efficiency of the search inside a bucket depends on the number of elements in it.","Hash tables are a fundamental data structure, ensuring fast access to elements by key. When two different keys give the same hash (a collision), they have to be held in one and the same place in the table, called a bucket. The chaining method is one of the most widespread ways of solving this problem.\\n\\nThe structure of a bucket with the chaining method\\n\\nWhen chaining is used, every bucket does not hold a single element but is the beginning of a linked list (or another linear container, a dynamic array for example). Every element whose key corresponds to the given hash is added to that list.\\n\\nThe algorithm of the search inside a bucket\\n\\nThe process of searching consists of two main steps:\\n\\n- Computing the index of the bucket: By means of the hash function of the key, a numeric index is computed, which points to the bucket needed in the table\'s array of buckets.\\n\\n- The linear search in the chain: Once the bucket has been obtained (that is, a pointer to the head of the linked list), a sequential pass over all of the nodes in that list begins. For every node it is checked whether the node\'s key is equal to the key sought.\\n\\nAn example of code in Python\\n\\nLet us look at a simplified implementation of a hash table with chaining and a search inside a bucket:\\n\\nIn this example the bucket is represented by an ordinary Python list. The `get` method computes the index, takes the corresponding list (`bucket`) and carries out a linear search over it, comparing the keys.\\n\\nPerformance and application\\n\\nIn the ideal case, when there are few collisions, every bucket contains 0 or 1 elements, and the search is carried out in O(1) time. In the worst case, when all of the keys fall into one bucket, the search degenerates into O(n), where n is the total number of elements. A high-quality hash function and a sufficient size of table are therefore critically important for efficiency.\\n\\nAn understanding of this mechanism is necessary for optimising performance in such areas as caching, databases (indexes) and associative arrays in programming languages (dictionaries in Python, objects in JavaScript, HashMap in Java).\\n\\nSummary: The search inside a bucket is a linear search over the chain of collisions. This approach is simple to implement and effective when the hash function distributes the keys evenly. It is worth applying everywhere a data structure with a fast average access time by key is required — namely in implementations of associative arrays, sets and caches.","class HashTable:\\n    def __init__(self, size=10):\\n        self.size = size\\n        self.buckets = [[] for _ in range(size)]  # Every bucket is a list (a chain)\\n\\n    def _hash(self, key):\\n        # The simplest hash function, for the demonstration\\n        return hash(key) % self.size\\n\\n    def get(self, key):\\n        \\"\\"\\"The search for a value by key.\\"\\"\\"\\n        bucket_index = self._hash(key)\\n        bucket = self.buckets[bucket_index]\\n        # The linear search inside the bucket (the list)\\n        for stored_key, value in bucket:\\n            if stored_key == key:\\n                return value  # The element has been found\\n        raise KeyError(f\\"Key \'{key}\' not found\\")  # The element has not been found\\n\\n    def put(self, key, value):\\n        \\"\\"\\"Adding or updating a key-value pair.\\"\\"\\"\\n        bucket_index = self._hash(key)\\n        bucket = self.buckets[bucket_index]\\n        # First we look to see whether such a key is already in the chain\\n        for i, (stored_key, _) in enumerate(bucket):\\n            if stored_key == key:\\n                bucket[i] = (key, value)  # Update the value\\n                return\\n        # If the key is not there, add a new element to the end of the chain\\n        bucket.append((key, value))\\n\\n# An example of use\\ntable = HashTable()\\ntable.put(\\"apple\\", 5)\\ntable.put(\\"banana\\", 7)\\nprint(table.get(\\"apple\\"))  # Output: 5. The search succeeded."],[693,"The map() method creates a new array by applying the function passed to every element of the original array. It does not change the original array but returns a new one with the transformed values. That is convenient for transforming data without side effects.","What is map()?\\nThe `map()` method is a built-in function of arrays in JavaScript, which lets you apply a callback to every element and obtain a new array of the same length. It belongs to the functional methods and does not mutate the original data.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe callback takes three arguments: the current element, its index and the array itself. The result of every call is added to the new array. Doubling numbers, for example:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- The transformation of data from an API (extracting fields, for example).\\n- Rendering lists in React.\\n- Converting types (strings into numbers).\\nConclusion\\nUse `map()` when you have to obtain a new array on the basis of an existing one without changing the original. That makes the code cleaner and more predictable.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2);\\n// doubled: [2, 4, 6]"],[694,"No, the operands during addition do not have to be of the same type. JavaScript brings them to a single type automatically. If one of the operands is a string, the other is converted into a string and concatenation happens. In the remaining cases both operands are converted into numbers and mathematical addition is carried out.","Type coercion during addition in JavaScript\\nIn JavaScript the `+` operator carries out two different operations: mathematical addition and the concatenation of strings. The choice of operation depends on the types of the operands. If at least one operand is a string, both are converted into strings and joined. Otherwise both operands are converted into numbers and added.\\nExamples\\n\\nThe order of conversion\\nJavaScript first checks whether there is a string among the operands. If there is, everything is converted into strings. If there is not, everything is converted into numbers. That can lead to unexpected results, especially with objects, which are converted through `valueOf` or `toString`.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of implicit type coercion matters for writing predictable code. It is recommended to convert types explicitly by means of `Number()`, `String()` or `Boolean()`, in order to avoid errors.","console.log(1 + 2); // 3 (numeric addition)\\nconsole.log(\'1\' + 2); // \'12\' (concatenation)\\nconsole.log(1 + \'2\'); // \'12\' (concatenation)\\nconsole.log(true + false); // 1 (true=1, false=0)\\nconsole.log(null + 1); // 1 (null=0)\\nconsole.log(undefined + 1); // NaN (undefined=NaN)"],[695,"Yes, an object can be both an iterable and an iterator at the same time. For that it has to implement the Symbol.iterator method returning this, and a next() method returning an object with the fields value and done. Such an object can be used in a for...of loop while managing its own iteration state.","An explanation of the concept\\nIn JavaScript there are two closely related protocols: iterable and iterator. An iterable is an object that defines the way of iterating by implementing the Symbol.iterator method. An iterator is an object with a next() method, which returns the next element of the sequence. Usually these are different objects, but technically a single object can combine both roles.\\nHow it works\\nFor an object to be an iterable, it has to have a Symbol.iterator method that returns an iterator. If that method returns the object itself (this), then the object becomes both an iterable and an iterator. It also has to implement a next() method with the correct logic.\\nAn example of code\\n\\nApplication in practice\\nSuch an approach is useful for creating simple custom iterators, when a separation of responsibility between the iterable object and the iterator is not required. It is worth remembering, however, that such an object can be used in only one iteration at a time, since the state of the iteration is held in the object itself.\\nConclusion\\nCombining an iterable and an iterator in one object is a permissible but niche technique. It is worth applying for simple scenarios where support for multiple simultaneous iterations is not needed and where the compactness of the code matters.","const customIterable = {\\n  current: 0,\\n  max: 5,\\n  [Symbol.iterator]() {\\n    return this; // we return ourselves as the iterator\\n  },\\n  next() {\\n    if (this.current < this.max) {\\n      return { value: this.current++, done: false };\\n    }\\n    return { value: undefined, done: true };\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfor (const num of customIterable) {\\n  console.log(num); // 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\n}"],[696,"If you change an attribute of an object used as a dictionary key, its hash value may change. That will break the dictionary\'s invariant, since the key will be located in the wrong bucket. As a result the dictionary may work incorrectly: a search by key may not find the element, or duplicates may arise. Only immutable objects should therefore be used as dictionary keys.","What happens when a dictionary key is changed?\\nDictionaries in Python (and analogous structures in other languages) use hash tables for fast access to data. A dictionary key must be hashable — that is, it must have an unchanging hash throughout the whole time it is used. If you change an attribute of an object that is a key, its hash value may change.\\nWhy is that a problem?\\nWhen you insert an element into a dictionary, Python computes the hash of the key and places it in the corresponding bucket. When searching for the key the hash is computed again, and the search goes in the same bucket. If the hash has changed after the insertion, the search will look in a different bucket, and the element will not be found. Moreover, the old hash may remain in the structure, which will lead to duplication or loss of data.\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nHow do you avoid the problem?\\n\\n- Use only immutable types as keys: strings, numbers, tuples (if their elements are immutable).\\n- If you have to use an object, make it immutable (through `__slots__` or `@dataclass(frozen=True)`, for example).\\n- Do not change an object after it has become a dictionary key.\\nConclusion\\nChanging an attribute of a key object breaks the contract of the hash table, which leads to unpredictable behaviour. Always use immutable objects as dictionary keys for the reliability and the predictability of the code.","class MutableKey:\\n    def __init__(self, value):\\n        self.value = value\\n    def __hash__(self):\\n        return hash(self.value)\\n    def __eq__(self, other):\\n        return self.value == other.value\\n\\nobj = MutableKey(10)\\nd = {obj: \\"original\\"}\\nprint(d[obj])  # \\"original\\"\\nobj.value = 20  # we change the attribute\\nprint(d.get(obj))  # None — the key has not been found\\nprint(d)  # the dictionary may look empty or contain a \\"lost\\" element"],[697,"The parameters of an HTTP request, such as the URL, the query parameters, the body of the request, the headers and the cookies, can be points of attack. Attackers can inject malicious data through these parameters, causing SQL injections, XSS, CSRF and other attacks. It therefore matters always to validate and sanitise the input data.","The parameters of an HTTP request as points of attack\\nHTTP requests contain a multitude of parameters that can be used by attackers to carry out attacks. The main points of entry include the URL, the query parameters, the body of the request, the headers and the cookies. Each of these elements can be manipulated in order to inject malicious code or data.\\nThe main vulnerabilities\\n\\n- SQL injections: The injection of SQL code through the parameters of a request — in the query parameters or the body of a POST request, for example.\\n- XSS (Cross-Site Scripting): The injection of JavaScript code through parameters that are displayed on a page without sanitisation.\\n- CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery): The use of cookies and headers to carry out actions on the user\'s behalf.\\n- Path Traversal: The manipulation of a URL to gain access to files outside the root directory.\\nAn example of code: protection against SQL injection\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo prevent attacks it is necessary always to validate and sanitise all of the input data, to use parameterised queries, to escape output and to apply CSRF tokens. That is critically important for the security of any web application.","// Node.js using parameterised queries\\nconst query = \'SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1\';\\nconst values = [req.query.id];\\ndb.query(query, values, (err, result) => {\\n  // handling the result\\n});"],[698,"To parse HTML in Python, BeautifulSoup, lxml and the built-in html.parser are used most often. BeautifulSoup is convenient for navigating the DOM tree, while lxml is faster and supports XPath. The choice depends on the task: for simple projects html.parser will do, for complex ones lxml or BeautifulSoup with lxml.","The main tools for parsing HTML in Python\\nParsing HTML is the process of extracting data from web pages. In Python there are several popular libraries, each of which has its own peculiarities. The main ones are: BeautifulSoup, lxml and the built-in html.parser.\\nBeautifulSoup\\nThis is a library for conveniently extracting data from HTML and XML. It creates an object representation of the document, letting you search for elements by tags, classes, IDs and attributes. BeautifulSoup can work with different parsers, including lxml and html.parser.\\n\\nlxml\\nThis is a fast, powerful parser supporting XPath and CSS selectors. It is written in C and is often used together with BeautifulSoup to speed up the work.\\n\\nhtml.parser\\nPython\'s built-in parser; it requires no additional libraries to be installed. It is slower than lxml, but it suits simple tasks.\\n\\nThe choice of tool depends on the requirements as regards speed and the complexity of the markup. For fast prototyping use BeautifulSoup with lxml; for highly loaded systems, pure lxml.","from bs4 import BeautifulSoup\\n\\nhtml = \'<div class=\\"content\\">Hello</div>\'\\nsoup = BeautifulSoup(html, \'html.parser\')\\nprint(soup.find(\'div\', class_=\'content\').text)  # Hello\\n\\nfrom lxml import html\\n\\ntree = html.fromstring(\'<div class=\\"content\\">Hello</div>\')\\nprint(tree.xpath(\'//div[@class=\\"content\\"]/text()\')[0])  # Hello\\n\\nfrom html.parser import HTMLParser\\n\\nclass MyParser(HTMLParser):\\n    def handle_data(self, data):\\n        print(data)\\n\\nparser = MyParser()\\nparser.feed(\'<div>Hello</div>\')  # Hello"],[699,"Validation checks whether data matches the rules given (the format of an email, for example). Sanitisation cleans or converts data in order to remove dangerous elements (HTML tags, for example). Validation answers the question \\"is the data correct?\\", while sanitisation answers \\"is the data safe?\\".","The main concepts\\nValidation and sanitisation are two key processes of handling data, especially when working with user input. Validation checks whether data matches particular criteria (the length of a string, the format of an email, a range of numbers, for example). Sanitisation, for its part, is aimed at removing or converting potentially dangerous parts of the data, such as HTML tags or SQL injections.\\nExamples and differences\\nImagine a registration form with a field for a username. Validation can check that the name contains only letters and digits and that its length is from 3 to 20 characters. Sanitisation will remove any HTML tags the user might have entered, in order to prevent XSS attacks.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Web forms (registration, comments)\\n- API endpoints (checking the input parameters)\\n- Databases (protection against SQL injections)\\nValidation is usually carried out first, in order to screen out data that is obviously incorrect. Sanitisation is applied afterwards, in order to make the data safe before saving or displaying it.\\nConclusion: Validation and sanitisation are mutually complementary practices. Validation guarantees that the data has the expected format, while sanitisation protects the system from malicious content. Use them together for the reliable handling of user input.","// An example of validation in JavaScript\\nfunction validateUsername(name) {\\n  const regex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]{3,20}$/;\\n  return regex.test(name);\\n}\\n\\n// An example of sanitisation (removing HTML tags)\\nfunction sanitizeInput(input) {\\n  return input.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, \'\');\\n}"],[700,"Parameterised queries are a way of carrying out SQL queries in which the structure of the query is separated from the data. Instead of inserting values directly into the string of the query, placeholders are used and the values are passed separately. That prevents SQL injections, since the database interprets the values passed only as data rather than as part of the code. Such queries can also be compiled in advance, which raises performance when they are carried out many times.","What are parameterised queries?\\nParameterised queries (prepared statements) are a mechanism for interacting with a database in which the SQL query is first sent to the database server for compilation with places indicated for the substitution of values (placeholders), and only then are the values themselves passed. That guarantees that the data passed will never be interpreted as part of the SQL command.\\nProtection against SQL injections\\nThe main reason for using parameterised queries is security. If you concatenate the string of a query with user input, an attacker can pass something like `\'; DROP TABLE users; --`. When placeholders are used, such input will be perceived as an ordinary string rather than as a command.\\nAn example in PHP (PDO)\\n\\nHere `:email` is the placeholder. Even if `$userInput` contains malicious SQL, it will not be carried out.\\nPerformance\\nWhen one and the same query is carried out repeatedly with different values, the database can reuse the compiled plan, which speeds up the work. That is especially useful in loops or during mass insertions.\\nConclusion\\nParameterised queries are a mandatory standard for any application working with a database. They protect against SQL injections and often improve performance. Use them always, whenever a query includes user data.","$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\'SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = :email\');\\n$stmt->execute([\'email\' => $userInput]);\\n$user = $stmt->fetch();"]]');export{e as default};